\subsubsection{The Expectation-Maximization for Unsupervised Artery Segmentation}

The Expectation-Maximization (EM) Algorithm \citep{zhang2001segmentation} is a robust statistical method used for parameter estimation in probabilistic models with latent variables. In the context of medical image segmentation, the EM is employed to refine the segmentation of a corrupted image by iteratively estimating class memberships and updating model parameters. To enhance spatial coherence, the EM algorithm integrates Iterated Conditional Modes (ICM) for maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimation, applied to a segmented image with a background mask. The algorithm-\ref{algo:em_segmentation} optimizes the segmentation by balancing data likelihood and spatial priors, controlled by a tunable penalty parameter.

% \subsubsection{Algorithm Overview}

The EM algorithm alternates between two steps: the Expectation (E) step, which computes the expected class memberships for each pixel, and the Maximization (M) step, which updates the model parameters (class means and standard deviations) based on these memberships. The implementation incorporates ICM to refine the segmentation by enforcing spatial consistency, guided by a Markov Random Field (MRF) prior. The algorithm terminates when the relative change in the log-posterior probability falls below a threshold or when the posterior decreases, indicating convergence or a suboptimal update.

The inputs to the algorithm include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \(\mathbf{img}\): The original 3D MRA image.
    \item \(\mathbf{seg}\): The initial segmented image, where each pixel is assigned a class label. (Prior is calculated from an initial constant threshold)
    \item \(\mathbf{mask}\): A binary background mask, where 1 indicates foreground pixels.
    \item \(k\): The number of classes. (Fixed as 2, foreground and background)
    \item \(\boldsymbol{\mu}\): Initial class means, \(\mu_i\) for class \(i \in \{1, \dots, k\}\).
    \item \(\boldsymbol{\sigma}\): Initial class standard deviations, \(\sigma_i\) for class \(i\).
    \item \(\beta\): A tunable penalty parameter for the MRF prior. (Fixed as 1)
    \item \(\epsilon_{\text{EM}}\): Stopping criterion for the EM algorithm.
    \item \(N_{\text{ICM}}\): Number of ICM iterations. (Configured based on dataset SNR-level, for our overall dataset we fixed it at 10)
    \item \(N_{\text{EM max}}\): Maximum number of EM iterations. (Configured based on dataset SNR, for our overall dataset it was fixed at 4)
\end{itemize}

The outputs are the updated segmented image \(\mathbf{seg}\), class memberships \(\mathbf{M}\), and updated parameters \(\boldsymbol{\mu}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\sigma}\).

% \subsubsection{Mathematical Formulation}

The EM algorithm seeks to maximize the log-posterior probability of the segmentation given the observed image, which combines a likelihood term and a prior term. For a pixel at position \((r, c)\), the observed intensity is \(y_{r,c} = \mathbf{img}(r,c)\), and the latent class label is \(z_{r,c} \in \{1, \dots, k\}\). The likelihood assumes a Gaussian distribution for each class:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    p(y_{r,c} | z_{r,c} = i, \mu_i, \sigma_i) &= \\ \mathcal{G}(y_{r,c}; \mu_i, \sigma_i) = 
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_i^2}} \exp\left(-\frac{(y_{r,c} - \mu_i)^2}{2\sigma_i^2}\right),
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where \(\mathcal{G}(y; \mu, \sigma)\) denotes the Gaussian probability density function.

The prior term is modeled using an MRF, which encourages spatial smoothness by penalizing dissimilar labels in neighboring pixels. The prior probability for a pixel \((r, c)\) belonging to class \(i\) is

\begin{equation}
    p(z_{r,c} = i | \mathbf{seg}, \beta) \propto \exp\left(-\beta \cdot U_{r,c}(i, \mathbf{seg})\right),
\end{equation}
where \(U_{r,c}(i, \mathbf{seg})\) is the prior penalty, typically the number of neighboring pixels with different labels, weighted by \(\beta\). The log-posterior probability can be computed as-

% \begin{equation*}
%     \log p(\mathbf{seg} | \mathbf{img}, \mathbf{mask}, \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}, \beta) = \sum_{r,c: \mathbf{mask}(r,c)=1} \left[ \log \mathcal{G}(\mathbf{img}(r,c); \mu_{z_{r,c}}, \sigma_{z_{r,c}}) - \beta U_{r,c}(z_{r,c}, \mathbf{seg}) \right].
% \end{equation*}

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \log p(\mathbf{seg} | \mathbf{img}, \mathbf{mask}, \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}, \beta) &= \\ \sum_{r,c: \mathbf{mask}(r,c)=1} \Big[ \log \mathcal{G}(\mathbf{img}(r,c); \mu_{z_{r,c}}, \sigma_{z_{r,c}})
    &\quad \\ - \beta U_{r,c}(z_{r,c}, \mathbf{seg}) \Big]
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The EM algorithm iteratively updates the segmentation and parameters to maximize this posterior.
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{EM Algorithm for Artery Segmentation\label{algo:em_segmentation}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{EM}{$\mathbf{seg}, \mathbf{img}, \mathbf{mask}, k, \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}, \beta, \epsilon_{\text{EM}}, N_{\text{ICM}}, N_{\text{EM max}}$}
    \State Initialize iteration counter $i_{\text{em}} \gets 0$
    \While{true}
        \State Compute $\log p_{\text{before}} \gets \log p(\mathbf{seg} | \mathbf{img}, \mathbf{mask}, \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}, \beta)$
        \State Update $\mathbf{seg} \gets \text{ICM}(\mathbf{seg}, \mathbf{img}, \mathbf{mask}, k, \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}, \beta, N_{\text{ICM}})$
        \State Compute $\log p_{\text{after}} \gets \log p(\mathbf{seg} | \mathbf{img}, \mathbf{mask}, \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}, \beta)$
        \State Compute $\text{rel\_change} \gets \frac{|\log p_{\text{after}} - \log p_{\text{before}}|}{|\log p_{\text{before}}|}$
        \If{$\text{rel\_change} \leq \epsilon_{\text{EM}}$ \textbf{or} $\log p_{\text{after}} < \log p_{\text{before}}$}
            \State \textbf{break}
        \EndIf
        \State Initialize membership array $\mathbf{M} \gets \text{zeros}(R, C, k)$
        \For{$r = 1$ to $R$}
            \For{$c = 1$ to $C$}
                \If{$\mathbf{mask}(r,c) = 1$}
                    \For{$i = 1$ to $k$}
                        \State Compute $m_{r,c,i} \gets \mathcal{G}(\mathbf{img}(r,c); \mu_i, \sigma_i) \cdot \exp(-\beta U_{r,c}(i, \mathbf{seg}))$
                    \EndFor
                    \State Normalize $\mathbf{M}(r,c,i) \gets \frac{m_{r,c,i}}{\sum_{j=1}^k m_{r,c,j}}$
                \EndIf
            \EndFor
        \EndFor
        \For{$i = 1$ to $k$}
            \State Compute $\mu_i \gets \frac{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i) \cdot \mathbf{img}(r,c)}{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i)}$
            \State Compute $\sigma_i^2 \gets \frac{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i) \cdot (\mathbf{img}(r,c) - \mu_i)^2}{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i)}$
            \State Set $\sigma_i \gets \sqrt{\sigma_i^2}$
        \EndFor
        \State Increment $i_{\text{em}} \gets i_{\text{em}} + 1$
        \If{$i_{\text{em}} \geq N_{\text{EM max}}$}
            \State \textbf{break}
        \EndIf
    \EndWhile
    \State \Return $\mathbf{seg}, \mathbf{M}, \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsubsection*{E-Step: Compute Class Memberships}

In the E-step, the algorithm computes the posterior probability (class membership) of each pixel \((r, c)\) belonging to class \(i\), given the current parameters and segmentation. For foreground pixels (\(\mathbf{mask}(r,c) = 1\)), the unnormalized membership is-

\begin{equation}
    m_{r,c,i} = \mathcal{G}(\mathbf{img}(r,c); \mu_i, \sigma_i) \cdot \exp\left(-\beta U_{r,c}(i, \mathbf{seg})\right).
\end{equation}

The memberships are normalized to ensure they sum to 1 over all classes:

\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{M}(r,c,i) = \frac{m_{r,c,i}}{\sum_{j=1}^k m_{r,c,j}}.
\end{equation}

Before computing memberships, the algorithm updates the segmentation using ICM, which iteratively assigns each pixel the label that maximizes the local posterior probability, considering both the likelihood and the MRF prior. ICM runs for \(N_{\text{ICM}}\) iterations, improving the MAP estimate of \(\mathbf{seg}\).

\subsubsection*{M-Step: Update Parameters}

In the M-step, the class means and standard deviations are updated using the memberships from the E-step. For each class \(i\), the updated mean is the weighted average of pixel intensities:

\begin{equation}
    \mu_i = \frac{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i) \cdot \mathbf{img}(r,c)}{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i)},
\end{equation}
where the summation is over all pixels \((r,c)\).

The updated variance is computed as:

\begin{equation}
    \sigma_i^2 = \frac{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i) \cdot (\mathbf{img}(r,c) - \mu_i)^2}{\sum_{r,c} \mathbf{M}(r,c,i)},
\end{equation}
and the standard deviation is \(\sigma_i = \sqrt{\sigma_i^2}\).

\subsubsection*{Convergence Check}

The algorithm evaluates convergence by computing the log-posterior probability before and after the ICM update. The relative change is:

\begin{equation}
    \text{rel\_change} = \frac{|\log p_{\text{after}} - \log p_{\text{before}}|}{|\log p_{\text{before}}|}.
\end{equation}

The algorithm terminates if \(\text{rel\_change} \leq \epsilon_{\text{EM}}\), if the posterior decreases (\(\log p_{\text{after}} < \log p_{\text{before}}\)), or if the maximum number of iterations \(N_{\text{EM max}}\) is reached.



The algorithm's convergence is sensitive to the choice of \(\epsilon_{\text{EM}}\) and \(\beta\). A small \(\epsilon_{\text{EM}}\) ensures precise convergence but may increase computational cost, while a large \(\beta\) enforces stronger smoothing, potentially oversmoothing fine details. The maximum iteration limit \(N_{\text{EM max}}\) prevents excessive computation in cases of slow convergence.

% \subsection*{Related Work}

The EM algorithm has been widely used in medical image segmentation, often combined with HMRF-EM priors to enforce spatial constraints \citep{zhang2001segmentation}, in our implementation a binary thresholding artery image initiates the EM and ICM iterations. The incorporation of ICM for MAP estimation is inspired by Julian\citep{besag1986statistical}, who introduced ICM as an efficient alternative to global optimization methods like simulated annealing. For a specific group of dataset that is obtained from the same scanner and protocol, that demonstrates similar SNR, we recommend to keep all the tunable parameters including the beta and number of iterations fixed. This addresses the inherent bias that originates from the using different contrast algorithms or parameters for segmentation.






\subsubsection{Isotropic Rasterization \& Geodesic Skeletonization}
Subsequently, the segmented data is standardized into an isotropic grid through resampling and rasterization, ensuring uniform voxel spacing for consistent analysis. The centerline and radius computation establishes a robust foundation for constructing a graph representation of the arterial network, critical for topological and quantitative vascular analyses.



\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Input Preparation:} The algorithm processes the 3D binary image where a unit structure specifying voxel dimensions along each axis ($x$, $y$, $z$). The voxels representing the arterial structure are resampled into isotropic voxel space to account for anisotropy, ensuring that distance calculations are physically accurate and corrected for the imaging system's spatial units.

    \item \textbf{Distance Transform Computation:} A distance transform is applied to the inverted binary image to compute the Euclidean distance from each voxel within the arterial structure to the nearest background voxel (i.e., close to the vessel wall). In the implementation, the \texttt{bwdist} function in MATLAB computes the Euclidean distance transform on the inverted binary image, where the arterial structure (originally foreground, intensity 1) is inverted to background (0), and non-vessel regions (originally 0) become foreground (1). This allows bwdist to calculate the straight-line (Euclidean) distance from each voxel inside the vessel to the nearest vessel wall voxel. The output is a distance map where each voxel’s value represents its Euclidean distance to the nearest background voxel with an index map identifying the linear index of the corresponding nearest background voxel.

    % A geodesic distance transform is applied to the inverted binary image to compute the distance from each voxel to the nearest background voxel (i.e., close to the vessel wall). Unlike standard Euclidean distance, geodesic distance considers the connectivity constraints within the arterial structure, providing a more accurate measure of the shortest path to the vessel boundary \cite{ref2}. This is implemented using a distance transform algorithm, similar to MATLAB's \texttt{bwdist}, which generates a distance map where each voxel's value represents its geodesic distance to the nearest background voxel, along with an index map identifying the corresponding nearest background voxel. The use of \texttt{bwdist} ensures computational efficiency, as it employs optimized algorithms to handle large 3D datasets, making it suitable for medical imaging applications.

    \item \textbf{Skeletonization:} The arterial structure is reduced to a one-voxel-thick centerline through 3D skeletonization, preserving the topological connectivity and branching structure of the arterial network. This centerline represents the medial axis of the vessels, serving as the reference for radius measurements. For the purpose of this study, iterative thinning technique was employed for skeletonizing the 3D tubular structures that ensured the centerline accurately reflects the vessel's geometry\cite{kollmannsberger_small_2017,lee_building_1994}.

    \item \textbf{Radius Computation:} The distance transform map and skeletonized 3D structure is combined to compute radius for centerline points. For each voxel on the centerline, the algorithm retrieves its 3D coordinates and identifies the nearest background voxel from the index map generated by the distance transform. The distance between the centerline voxel and its nearest background voxel is calculated, adjusted for the anisotropic voxel dimensions. For a centerline voxel at position $(x_i, y_i, z_i)$ with the nearest background voxel at $(d_x, d_y, d_z)$, the radius is computed as:
    \begin{equation}
    r = \sqrt{\begin{aligned}
        &((x_i - d_x) \cdot \text{unit.x})^2 + \\
        &((y_i - d_y) \cdot \text{unit.y})^2 + \\
        &((z_i - d_z) \cdot \text{unit.z})^2
    \end{aligned}}
    \end{equation}
    This radius value represents the shortest path from the centerline close to the vessel wall, providing a precise measure of the vessel's radial extent at that point. Supplementary Fig. S1 shows the distance transform (in pixels) maps computed for 3 different test cases in 2D.

    \item \textbf{Centerline Output:} The computed radii represented and stored in a 3D variable, with non-zero values assigned only to centerline voxels takes extensive memory and processing time. Sparse representation of this data is used for all further processing and vessel fusing algorithm.

\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection*{Implementation Details}
The algorithm is designed for computational efficiency and accuracy, leveraging vectorized operations and optimized libraries for distance transform computation and skeletonization. The distance transform, inspired by the functionality of \texttt{bwdist}, is particularly effective for 3D medical imaging datasets, as it scales well with image size and handles complex geometries. The incorporation of unit corrections ensures that radial measurements are physically accurate, addressing challenges posed by non-uniform voxel spacing in modalities such as Magnetic Resonance Angiography (MRA). The resulting centerline and diameter data are stored in a sparse format compatible with downstream graph generation processes.

\subsubsection{Create \& Label Vessel-Fused Network Nodes}

\begin{algorithm*}
\caption{Vessel-Fuse Network Creation\label{algo:vesselfused}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{VesselFusedNetworkCreation}{$\text{Skeleton3D}$}
    \State $\text{Endpoints}, \text{Nodes}, \text{Hubs} \gets \text{IdentifySkeletonComponents}(\text{Skeleton3D})$ \Comment{Identify endpoints, nodes, hubs}
    \State Initialize $\text{VesselFusedNetwork}$ with empty $\text{GlobalNodes}, \text{GlobalTraces}$
    \For{each $\text{Hub}$ in $\text{Hubs}$}
        \State $\text{MiniSubgraph} \gets \text{CreateMiniSubgraph}(\text{Hub})$ \Comment{Create subgraph for hub}
        \State $\text{HubCenterNode} \gets \text{IdentifyHubCenterNode}(\text{MiniSubgraph})$ \Comment{Identify central node}
        \State $\text{HubSubTraces} \gets \text{IdentifyHubSubTraces}(\text{MiniSubgraph}, \text{HubCenterNode})$ \Comment{Paths from endpoints to center}
        \State $\text{VesselFusedNetwork}.\text{GlobalNodes} \gets \text{VesselFusedNetwork}.\text{GlobalNodes} \cup \text{MiniSubgraph}.\text{Nodes}$
        \State $\text{VesselFusedNetwork}.\text{GlobalTraces} \gets \text{VesselFusedNetwork}.\text{GlobalTraces} \cup \text{HubSubTraces}$
    \EndFor
    \State \Return $\text{VesselFusedNetwork}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm*}

