%\newpage
\paragraph*{Case 3b.}
$a$ and $b$  are on the same side of $lr$, ({\em wlog,} below; and also 
%$a$ and $b$ lie on the same side of $lr$. After suitable reflections, we may assume that $a$ and $b$ lie below $lr$, 
  $a$ is to the left of $b$).
  By convexity, the  intersection point, $x$, of $\lin{l}{a}$ and $\lin{r}{b}$ is in the slab.
If $l'$ lies above $\lin{l}{c}$,  as in Figure~\ref{convex4-2-fig}(a),     we set $\Delta=\delta\oplus(l',c,l,x,r,r')$.
%
%
Symmetrically, if $r'$ lies above $\lin{r}{c}$, then we set $\Delta=\delta\oplus(l',l,x,r,c,r')$.
%
It remains to consider the case when $l'$ is below $\lin{l}{c}$ and $r'$ is below $\lin{r}{c}$. We consider two subcases.  


