\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:exp}

This section presents an experimental evaluation of existing brain visual decoding methods across multiple benchmark datasets. Experimental Details (\cref{subsec:experimental_details}) introduces the datasets, decoding methods, implementation settings, and additional evaluation protocols, while Experimental Results (\cref{subsec:experimental_results}) reports the quantitative performance using our proposed evaluation framework.



\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:experimental_details}


\vspace{-2.5mm}\paragraph{Stimulus-neuroimaging datasets and methods.}
\label{subsec:datasets}

We benchmark brain visual decoding methods across six representative datasets spanning three major visual modalities (images, videos, and 3D shapes ) and two neuroimaging techniques (fMRI and EEG). 
These datasets include: the fMRI-image dataset NSD, which has been used in various decoding methods for visual image reconstruction~\cite{takagi2023improving,ozcelik2023brain,scotti2023reconstructing,scotti2024mindeye2,xia2024dream,xia2024umbrae,shen2024neuro}; the fMRI-video dataset CC2017~\cite{wen2018neural}, which has been employed in methods exploring dynamic stimuli decoding~\cite{li2025decofuse,chen2023cinematic,huo2024neuropictor}; and the fMRI-3D dataset MinD-3D~\cite{gao2024mind3d}, focusing on shape-level decoding from object stimuli, with related methods examining brain-based shape reconstruction~\cite{gao2024mind3d,gao2024mind3d++}. EEG-based datasets such as EEG-Things~\cite{grootswagers2022human}, SEED-DV~\cite{liu2024eeg2video}, and EEG-3D~\cite{guo2025neuro3d} have been used to decode brain activities for images~\cite{grootswagers2022human,li2025decofuse,chen2023cinematic,huo2024neuropictor}, videos~\cite{liu2024eeg2video}, and 3D shapes~\cite{guo2025neuro3d}. 
For each dataset, we evaluate all available brain visual decoding methods , as acknowledged, with open-source implementations or author-shared reconstruction results within our unified evaluation framework. 
These methods represent the state of the art in brain visual decoding and reflect recent methodological trends using pretrained generative models~\cite{radford2021learning,rombach2022high}.
This coverage enables standardized and multigranular comparisons across the current landscape of brain visual decoding research. 
Further details on datasets and methods are in~\cref{sec:supmat_dataset} and~\cref{sec:supmat_evaluation}.

\vspace{-2.5mm}\paragraph{Implementation details.}

LLaVA-1.6-13B~\cite{liu2023visual} is set as the default MLLM for detailed description generation and semantic instance categorization. Rather than using GPT-4o or other larger but more capable models, we opt LLaVA-1.6-13B as a practical trade-off between captioning accuracy and computational efficiency.
Ground truth captions are obtained from human experts through error correction, missing element addition, and hallucination removal, based on GPT-4o-generated captions, for better alignment with human judgment.
For multigranular segmentation, we employ Grounded-SAM2~\cite{ren2024grounded}, an open-source referring expression comprehension method, to obtain foreground, semantic, instance, and part-level segmentation masks. 
The default weights $\alpha_1$, $\alpha_2$, and $\alpha_3$ for BASIC-H, and $\beta_1$, $\beta_2$, $\beta_3$ and $\beta_4$ for BASIC-L, are set to 4:4:2 and 3:2.5:2.5:2, respectively, reflecting the empirically informed importance of each sub-indicator in capturing semantic and structural alignment.
Experiments are conducted on an NVIDIA A100 GPU. 
To ensure broad applicability and methodological consistency, we refrain from using MLLMs or segmentation methods specifically designed for video or 3D data. 
Instead, we retain an image-based framework with  automatic selection of representative video frames and rendered 3D views that best capture key semantics and structure from decoded reconstructions and corresponding stimuli~\cite{guo2025neuro3d,gao2024mind3d,liu2024eeg2video,wen2018neural}.

\vspace{-.8em}
\subsection{Experimental Results}
\label{subsec:experimental_results}
\vspace{-.5em}

This section presents experimental results showcasing the effectiveness of our proposed framework for brain visual decoding evaluation. The dual-perspective framework enables fine-grained and holistic comparisons, revealing method capabilities, limitations, and future development implications.


Quantitative evaluation for fMRI-to-image reconstruction on NSD~\cite{allen2022massive} following standard NSD and BASIC metrics is in~\cref{tab:quantitative_nsd_eeg_things}. As shown, while NSD metrics saturates in scores and fails to distinguish method performance, our metrics provide clearer differentiation and more discriminative information across objects, attributes, and relations for high-level semantics, as well as salient, semantic, instance, and part granularity for low-level structure. Details for each dimension are in~\cref{tab:basic_h} and~\cref{tab:basic_l}.

\vspace{-2.5mm}\paragraph{Semantics: object, attribute, and relation.} \cref{tab:basic_h} presents a comparison of object, attribute, and relation prediction performance across several datasets. 
While prior metrics often produce saturated or undifferentiated scores across models (see~\cref{sec:supmat_evaluation}), our proposed multigranular evaluation reveals meaningful variations in object coherence, attribute accuracy, and relational plausibility, offering a more fine-grained diagnostic lens.
Here, for methods on NSD~\cite{allen2022massive}, NeuroVLA~\cite{shen2024neuro}, DREAM~\cite{xia2024dream}, and STTM~\cite{liu2025see} achieve the highest BASIC-H scores (47.88, 46.37, and 45.88, respectively), reflecting their strength in modeling rich visual semantics. This strength likely stems from detailed caption generation, complex visual semantics modeling, cross-subject training, and the combination of these factors. A closer comparison between NeuroVLA and DREAM shows that while both models reconstruct the correct instances (achieving high precision) across the three semantic dimensions, NeuroVLA misses fewer relevant instances (better recall).
In contrast, SDRecon~\cite{takagi2023improving}, BrainDiffuser~\cite{ozcelik2023brain}, and UniBrain~\cite{wang2024unibrain} lag behind with BASIC-H scores of 35.31, 39.71, and 39.89, respectively. These methods struggle notably with the identification of object categories, attributes, and relations, indicating limitations in handling semantics or generalizing across visual categories.

\vspace{-2.5mm}\paragraph{Structure: salient, semantic, instance, and part.} 
\cref{tab:basic_l} reports performance across salient, binary, semantic, instance, and part segmentation matching scores. For decoding methods on NSD~\cite{allen2022massive}, NeuroPictor~\cite{huo2024neuropictor} leads with the highest BASIC-L score of 25.88, driven by its superior performance in instance and part segmentation. This reflects its ability to reconstruct fine-grained visual details and delineate object boundaries from neural signals.
STTM~\cite{liu2025see} and MindEye2~\cite{scotti2024mindeye2} also perform competitively, with BASIC-L scores of 22.90 and 22.16, respectively. SDRecon~\cite{takagi2023improving} and NeuroVLA~\cite{shen2024neuro}, with BASIC-L scores of 11.81 and 13.54, respectively, show relatively poor spatial structural fidelity.
BrainGuard~\cite{tian2025brainguard} achieves competitive scores in salient and semantic categories but performs less satisfactorily in instance and part segmentation. This indicates that while the model captures the overall structure of salient objects and categories, it faces challenges in distinguishing between multiple instances within the same category and identifying sub-component relationships.

