The core idea behind IOLM-DB is to integrate workload-driven optimization of LLMs into the query execution pipeline of OLAP systems. By continuously observing query workloads and identifying recurring patterns, the system dynamically optimizes LLMs through pruning and quantization techniques, resulting in smaller, task-specialized models. This section elaborates on the techniques utilized, including quantization, sparsification, and structural pruning, as well as their application within IOLM-DB.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsection{Quantization Techniques}
Quantization reduces the precision of weights and activations to decrease memory consumption and computational overhead. We employ the following approaches:
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsubsection{Activation Quantization: FP8}

This optimization reduces the number of bits used to represent weights and activations from 16 to 8, reducing GPU memory requirements (by approximately 50\%) and increasing matrix-multiply compute throughput (by approximately 2x). Weight quantization also reduces disk size requirements by approximately 50\%.

Only weights and activations of the linear operators within transformers blocks are quantized. Weights are quantized with a symmetric static per-channel scheme, where a fixed linear scaling factor is applied between FP8 and BF16 representations for each output channel dimension. Linear scaling factors are computed by minimizing the mean squared error (MSE). Activations are quantized with a symmetric dynamic per-token scheme, computing a linear scaling factor at runtime for each token between FP8 and BF16 representations.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsubsection{Mixed Precision: W8A16}
Mixed-precision quantization uses lower precision (8 bits) for weights while retaining higher precision (16 bits) for activations. This optimization reduces the number of bits per parameter from 16 to 4, reducing the disk size and GPU memory requirements. Only the weights of the linear operators within transformers blocks are quantized. Symmetric per-channel quantization is applied, in which a linear scaling per output dimension maps the INT4 and floating point representations of the quantized weights, This technique mitigates the accuracy loss typically associated with aggressive quantization while retaining performance gains.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsection{Sparsification Techniques}

Sparsification introduces zero elements into model weights or activations to reduce memory and computational requirements. 

IOLM-DB employs the following sparsification strategies:
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsubsection{2:4 Semi-structured Sparsity}
This technique enforces a structured sparsity pattern where, in every group of four weights, at least two are zero. Semi-structured sparsity leverages hardware accelerations designed for such patterns, resulting in faster inference.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsubsection{Unstructured Sparsity}
Unstructured sparsity removes weights with negligible values without adhering to predefined patterns. While less hardware-friendly compared to semi-structured sparsity, it provides greater flexibility in reducing model size.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsection{Integration of Quantization and Sparsification}
To maximize efficiency, IOLM-DB integrates sparsification and quantization using the well-known methods:
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsubsection{GPTQ (Quantization-Aware Pruning)}
GPTQ combines pruning with quantization by adapting the remaining weights to minimize accuracy loss. This method is particularly effective for large-scale models, enabling significant reductions in model size while maintaining performance.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsubsection{SmoothQuant}
SmoothQuant introduces smooth scaling factors during quantization to balance weight distributions. By smoothing activation outliers, it facilitates accurate and efficient post-training quantization for LLMs, enabling 8-bit weight and activation quantization without additional training.

\subsubsection{SparseGPT}
SparseGPT is a pruning method specifically designed for LLMs. It enables one-shot pruning to significant sparsity levels (e.g., 50\%) without retraining, maintaining model accuracy. SparseGPT is compatible with both unstructured and semi-structured sparsity patterns and can be combined with quantization approaches for further efficiency gains.

\subsection{Calibration Data Usage}
Calibration data, a small set of unlabeled examples, is crucial for post-training quantization and pruning methods. It assists in generating layer activations, enabling the adjustment of quantization parameters and pruning thresholds to better fit the specific data distribution, thereby preserving model performance. Moreover, the calibration data helps make these models specialized for the specific tasks they are optimized for, enhancing their efficiency and accuracy for analytics workloads. The effectiveness of calibration data in model compression has been systematically investigated, highlighting its impact on downstream task performance.

\subsection{Full Structural Pruning}
\textbf{LLM-Pruner} is employed for full structural pruning in IOLM-DB. This technique removes entire attention heads, feedforward network components, or layers that contribute minimally to the model's performance. By focusing on structural elements, LLM-Pruner enables significant reductions in model size while maintaining task-specific functionality. This approach is particularly advantageous for OLAP workloads with well-defined query patterns.

\subsection{Workflow Integration}
The mentioned optimization techniques are seamlessly integrated into the query execution pipeline. As queries are executed, the system collects workload statistics and identifies recurring patterns. Based on these observations, a smaller, instance-optimized LLM is generated, leveraging quantization, sparsification, and pruning. This process ensures the continual adaptation of the model to evolving workloads, balancing efficiency and accuracy in OLAP systems.

