	The plots in Fig.~\ref{fig:prob1_6_21} illustrate the RIS gain as a function of the azimuth angle $\phi \in [-80^{\circ},80^{\circ}]$ for a steering direction $\phi_{\rm UE} = -80^{\circ}$, along with the corresponding minimum gain in this configuration. It is observed that the RIS gain attains its minimum value, namely, $G_{\rm RIS,min} = 70.625$ dB, at approximately $\phi_q = -76^{\circ}$. This angle is then used to compute the subsequent steering direction, as outlined in Algorithm 1. The same procedure is iteratively applied to determine the successive angle $\phi_{q+1}$ and the associated phase-shift matrix $\mathbf{\Psi}_{q+1}$, as described in Algorithm 1, continuing until the steering direction reaches $\phi_{\rm UE}=80^{\circ}$.
