	Figure~\ref{fig67} shows the results obtained by applying the proposed methods (Algorithm 1 and Algorithm 2) for the three deployment cases under consideration. The threshold values used are respectively $\psi_{\rm th} = 30^{\circ}$ and $31^{\circ}$, $24^{\circ}$ and $29^{\circ}$, $31^{\circ}$ and $28^{\circ}$. The figure demonstrates a strong correlation along the columns of the RIS, suggesting that the elements within each column can be grouped together and controlled using a single control signal. This implies that, for effective beamforming in the desired direction, it is sufficient to configure the phase shifts of the elements in the first row of the RIS. This leads to the proposed RIS structure, namely, the Connected-RIS. This structure ensures that the imposed phase pattern propagates consistently across the remaining elements in each column of the surface.	
