\begin{abstract}

Phishing attacks are becoming increasingly sophisticated, underscoring the need for detection systems that strike a balance between high accuracy and computational efficiency. This paper presents a comparative evaluation of traditional Machine Learning (ML), Deep Learning (DL), and quantized small-parameter Large Language Models (LLMs) for phishing detection. Through experiments on a curated dataset, we show that while LLMs currently underperform compared to ML and DL methods in terms of raw accuracy, they exhibit strong potential for identifying subtle, context-based phishing cues. We also investigate the impact of zero-shot and few-shot prompting strategies, revealing that LLM-rephrased emails can significantly degrade the performance of both ML and LLM-based detectors. Our benchmarking highlights that models like DeepSeek R1 Distill Qwen 14B (Q8\_0) achieve competitive accuracy, above 80\%, using only 17~GB of VRAM, supporting their viability for cost-efficient deployment. We further assess the models' adversarial robustness and cost-performance tradeoffs, and demonstrate how lightweight LLMs can provide concise, interpretable explanations to support real-time decision-making. These findings position optimized LLMs as promising components in phishing defence systems and offer a path forward for integrating explainable, efficient AI into modern cybersecurity frameworks.

\end{abstract}


\begin{comment}
Cybersecurity threats are continually intensifying, especially as phishing attacks are becoming more complex, showing the need for detection systems that are capable of delivering high accuracy while managing computational demands effectively. This review paper offers a comparative analysis of traditional Machine Learning (ML) and Deep Learning (DL) methods, validated through our own experimental evaluations on a curated phishing dataset alongside quantized, small parameter Large Language Models (LLMs). While the results from the LLMs do not yet match the accuracy of the ML and DL techniques on this dataset, they do have significant potential for detecting subtle phishing cues that other phishing detection methods might otherwise miss. Our paper also examines the impact of zero-shot and few-shot prompting on phishing email detection, highlighting literature evidence that automated LLM rephrasing of emails can lead to decreased detection accuracy in ML and LLM-based systems. Our comprehensive evaluation is supported by detailed performance metrics, demonstrating that models such as DeepSeek R1 Distill Qwen 14B (Q8\_0) are able to achieve notable accuracy levels approaching 80\%, while using only around 17 GB of VRAM, which is substantially lower than full-scale LLMs. Overall, the findings show the promise of optimized LLMs as a versatile addition to real-time phishing detection systems and lay the groundwork for future research to include further improvements to their capabilities through specialized training with updated datasets and lightweight integration into modern cybersecurity frameworks.
\end{comment}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Phishing Attacks Detection, Large Language Models (LLMs), Cybersecurity, Deep Learning (DL), Machine Learning (ML)  
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Phishing is a prevalent cyberthreat that manipulates users into divulging sensitive information, such as credentials and financial data. Between November 2023 and January 2024, the Cybercrime Information Center collected approximately one million phishing reports \cite{putra2024analysis}. With the increasing sophistication of phishing tactics, traditional detection approaches struggle to keep pace with evolving attack techniques. Phishing has become more challenging in the 2020s due to the increased use of social media and multi-vector attacks that combine various methods, including email, text messages, and social media platforms. They have evolved from simple, deceptive emails to highly sophisticated social engineering schemes. Attackers leverage Artificial Intelligence (AI) generated content, deepfake techniques, and multi-channel deception strategies to exploit human vulnerabilities. Integration of AI, particularly Machine Learning (ML) and Natural Language Processing (NLP), has shown promising results in alleviating some of this burden. Several recent studies published between 2021 and 2024 have demonstrated the effectiveness of deep learning models, including Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) \cite{otoum2024advancing}, in detecting phishing attempts with higher accuracy compared to classical approaches, with XGBoost achieving the highest accuracy at 99.89\%, followed by PILFER with 99.5\% \cite{alghenaim2025state}. However, recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) and Generative AI (GenAI) \cite{otoum2025llm, otoum2025llms} further exacerbate the threat, enabling automated and highly targeted phishing campaigns. The authors of \cite{afane2024nextgen} found that while traditional phishing detectors, such as Gmail Spam Detector, SpamAssassin, Proofpoint, and State-of-the-Art LLMs perform well on original phishing emails, their accuracy and recall decline notably when dealing with LLM-rephrased versions of the duplicate emails. The study displayed a significantly better classification performance from LLMs with rephrased emails, and even with the original emails, the performance was still marginally more accurate. Unfortunately, the performance gains of LLMs come at a significant cost in terms of energy and computational resources. The total energy consumption for training a transformer model with 6B parameters to completion is estimated to be around $103.5 MWh$ \cite{dodge2022carbon}, and given that the computational cost of training an LLM depends on the number of parameters, modern alternatives like GPT-1, GPT-2, and GPT-3 featuring 117 million, 1.5 billion, and 175 billion parameters, respectively, the question arises whether the marginal performance gain is worth it. And if and how the LLMs or Traditional approaches can be evolved to provide a more optimal solution. This paper presents a structured review of such next-generation phishing detection techniques and their cost-to-performance ratio, focusing on the following contributions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A comparative analysis of traditional, Machine Learning, Deep Learning, and LLM-based phishing detection approaches.
    \item Exploration of adversarial phishing tactics, LLM rephrasing, and AI/LLM-driven countermeasures.
    \item Comparative analysis of model performances and marginal performance gain against supplemental time and resource consumption.
    \item Identification of research gaps and future directions in phishing detection.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section~\ref{Literature Review} reviews related works across ML, DL, and LLM-based phishing detection, identifying key research gaps. Section~\ref{Proposed Model} presents the proposed models and methodologies, including experimental setup and dataset details. Section~\ref{CONCLUSION} summarizes the findings, highlights cost-performance trade-offs, and outlines future research directions for robust, efficient phishing detection.



\begin{comment}


\section{Background and Fundamental Concepts}
\label{Background and Fundamental Concepts}

Phishing remains a pervasive cyber threat that exploits social engineering to extract sensitive data from unsuspecting users \cite{altwaijry2024dlmodels}. Although malicious emails historically dominated the landscape, newer research indicates that attackers now employ multiple vectors, including domain spoofing, phone calls (vishing), text messages (smishing), and AI-assisted tactics \cite{tamal2024unveiling}. These schemes often succeed by exploiting limited user awareness and high trust in persuasive communication channels.

\subsection{Traditional Methods for Phishing Detection}

Conventional defences typically involve tracking known malicious domains, analyzing straightforward indicators like URL length, or relying on rule-based checks \cite{hassan2022hybrid}. These approaches, while helpful in identifying foundational phishing traits, often fail when faced with novel or dynamic attack strategies:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Blocklists and Simple Heuristics:} Static checks can detect basic scams but are easily bypassed by attackers rotating URLs or using slight modifications.
    \item \textit{Manually Extracted Features:} Feature-driven machine learning models flag some patterns but lack adaptability to sophisticated or evolving techniques.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{AI-Driven Improvements}
In response to increasingly sophisticated attacks, researchers have applied machine learning and deep learning to detect patterns in phishing content \cite{ozgur2017machine}. Approaches include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Classical Machine Learning:} Algorithms such as Random Forests and Support Vector Machines (SVMs) detect structural anomalies across large datasets.
    \item \textit{Deep Learning Models:} Visual and semantic features, extracted using CNNs and related architectures, improve deceptive design or language detection.
    \item \textit{Context-Aware and Multimodal Analysis:} Recent work explores combining HyperText Markup Language (HTML) content, screenshots, and domain matching through LLMs, showing promising robustness in brand recognition and phishing page classification \cite{kulkarni2024robustness}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Adaptive Threats and Human Factors}

Modern phishing campaigns use dynamic strategies like morphing email content, altering logos, or leveraging homograph attacks to evade detection. Traditional models struggle when adversarial phishing elements, such as visual alterations or cloaked behaviours, are introduced. Moreover, phishing detection systems are frequently undermined by human error, as evidenced in studies where nearly half of the simulated phishing pages deceived users \cite{kulkarni2024robustness}.

\subsection{Integrated and Proactive Defences}
Future defences are shifting toward holistic, scalable, and adaptive solutions:
\begin{itemize}

\item \textit{Phishing Page Generation for Testing:} Tools like PhishOracle enable the automated creation of adversarial phishing webpages to evaluate and strengthen detection model robustness \cite{kulkarni2024robustness}.

\item \textit{LLM-Powered Education and Warnings:} Language models can personalize phishing simulations, training modules, and real-time warnings based on user profiles, improving awareness and reducing susceptibility \cite{greco2024supporting}.

\item \textit{Policy Automation and Governance:} Enterprise-scale AI integration enables real-time adjustment of security policies, particularly in high-risk contexts like executive-level targeting \cite{quinn2024applying}.

\end{itemize}

The following sections comprehensively evaluate these AI-driven defences and highlight key performance differentials and open research challenges.
