\subsection{Related Works}

The evolution of phishing detection has seen a significant shift from conventional ML/NLP approaches to more complex analytics using Deep Learning models, and recently to adopting new LLMs. Bethany et al. \cite{bethany2024llm} show that LLM-generated spear phishing emails can mimic human-crafted messages to a high degree, which creates another layer of challenges for legacy detection systems. Complementary research by Kulkarni et al. \cite{kulkarni2024robustness} demonstrates that LLM-based detectors retain higher performance under adversarial conditions than traditional models. Recent studies have also explored the integration of LLMs into Security Awareness Training (SAT) \cite{is2024llm}, and cybersecurity policy formulation \cite{quinn2024applying}, which shows that these methods can reduce phishing susceptibility and enable agile responses to evolving threats from phishing attacks. Transformer-based architectures such as BERT and GPT have demonstrated significant potential in enhancing phishing detection. For instance, an approach for early detection of malicious prompts to prevent LLMs from generating phishing content was proposed by Roy et. al. \cite{roy2024chatbots} It is reported to be transferable across all four commercial LLMs, namely ChatGPT (GPT 3.5 Turbo), GPT 4, Claude, and Bard, and attained an average accuracy of 96\% for phishing website prompts and 94\% for phishing email prompts. Although ML and NLP techniques \cite{kyaw2024systematic, thakur2023sys} provided essential insights into early phishing detection, they cannot cope with the dynamic nature of modern phishing attacks. This review motivates the development of a hybrid framework that combines the contextual strengths of LLMs with the proven reliability of conventional phishing detection methods. 
