    \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/clf_performance.pdf}
    \caption[]{\textbf{Designing the Learning Task:} 
Five-fold cross-validation accuracy for various modeling strategies used to formulate the learning task.
The model labeled with $1$ is trained using directly discretized labels in the space \(\boldsymbol{y} \in \{0,1\}^{K(n)}\), whereas those labeled with $3$ use structured labels in $\boldsymbol{Y} \in \{0,1\}^{s \times (n+1)}$. 
$C1$ and $C3$ denote standard classification models with one and three output heads, respectively.
$C3r$ and $C3re$ are chained (recursive) models, with $C3re$ representing an ensemble of such chains.
The results compare the predictive performance of different classification formulations discussed in the text.
All models are trained to predict pole configurations across the $[bt]$, $[tb]$, and $[bb]$ regions, using either shared or factorized label representations accordingly.
