\begin{abstract}
With the increased deployment of large language models (LLMs), one concern is their potential misuse for generating harmful content. Our work studies the \textit{alignment} challenge, with a focus on {\em filters} to prevent the generation of unsafe information. Two natural points of intervention are the filtering of the input prompt before it reaches the model, and filtering the output after generation. Our main results demonstrate computational challenges in filtering both prompts and outputs. First, we show that there exist LLMs for which there are no efficient prompt filters: adversarial prompts that elicit harmful behavior can be easily constructed, which are computationally indistinguishable from benign prompts for any efficient filter. Our second main result identifies a natural setting in which output filtering is computationally intractable. All of our separation results are under cryptographic hardness assumptions. In addition to these core findings, we also formalize and study relaxed mitigation approaches, demonstrating further computational barriers.
We conclude that safety cannot be achieved by designing filters external to the LLM internals (architecture and weights); in particular, black-box access to the LLM will not suffice.
Based on our technical results, we argue that an aligned AI system’s intelligence cannot be separated from its judgment.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems, particularly large language models (LLMs), are being adopted across a wide array of domains, including business, healthcare, education, and even governance \citep{potash2015predictive, chiusi2020automating, 
levy2021algorithms,
haensch2023seeing,  perdomo2023difficult, fischer2024bridging}. %These systems are becoming integral to decision-making processes, powering many applications from customer service to legal analysis and policy recommendations. 
As the influence of AI expands, ensuring the safety, reliability, and alignment of these systems with human values has become a critical societal concern. Governments and regulatory bodies around the globe are responding to this challenge by introducing frameworks to classify, monitor, and audit AI systems. 
For instance, the European Union’s AI Act \citep{euai} mandates extensive risk assessments and management for high-risk AI applications.
%, aiming to ensure that these systems are not only effective but also aligned with ethical and legal standards.  

