Informally, alignment refers to the process of ensuring that a model generates outputs that are consistent with human preferences, essentially teaching the model to generate responses that align with what humans consider safe and beneficial \citep{amodei2016concrete, leike2018scalable, bai2022training}. Despite the critical importance of this goal, the question remains: can we hope to guarantee the safety and alignment of advanced AI systems or are there intrinsic barriers to such guarantees? %While technical progress in areas such as reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) and interpretability has made strides toward reducing risks, real-world challenges persist and foundational questions remain unanswered. 

In this paper, we investigate fundamental limitations to achieving AI alignment through the approach of \textit{filtering} (see Figure~\ref{fig:braininavat}). Because many generative-AI models are proprietary and cannot be independently audited and thus trusted, filter-based alignment is an important subject of study.
Under standard cryptographic assumptions, we show that significant computational barriers exist to filtering both input prompts and output responses of LLMs in order to prevent harmful content.

Our contributions are twofold. First, for \emph{prompt filtering}, we construct LLMs for which no efficient filter can distinguish adversarial prompts—crafted to elicit harmful behavior—from benign ones. These prompts are computationally indistinguishable for any efficient detector, implying an inherent barrier to blocking harmful inputs.
Second, we identify natural settings where \emph{output filtering} is also computationally intractable. Even when a prompt is deemed safe, determining whether the resulting output is harmful may itself be infeasible for any efficient filter. This suggests that both preemptive (input-based) and reactive (output-based) filtering can face insurmountable computational challenges.

Furthermore, we formalize and analyze relaxed mitigation strategies in which filters are allowed to modify prompts or outputs, rather than simply rejecting them. Although these \emph{mitigation filters} have greater expressive power, we show that they, too, encounter fundamental computational barriers, indicating that even these more flexible approaches are subject to inherent limitations.

Taken together, our results reveal deep challenges in achieving provably aligned AI systems via standard filtering techniques. These findings underscore the need for a more rigorous understanding of the computational hardness underlying alignment—and raise important implications for the design of regulatory frameworks and technical safeguards.

From a philosophical perspective, we argue that \emph{intelligence} cannot be separated from \emph{judgment}. 
An AI---like the brain-in-a-vat in Figure~\ref{fig:braininavat}---\emph{cannot be aligned externally},\footnote{In computational complexity terms, this corresponds to alignment with only black-box access to the model.} by filters that assess the harmfulness of prompts and outputs.


\subsection{Prompt Filtering} 

The first approach we consider is running user prompts through a {\em prompt filter}, which can detect and discard prompts that might lead to harmful answers from the LLM. We focus on a setting in which the LLM itself is untrusted, but we can query it while training a prompt filter (during training we can try different prompts to observe if and where harmful behaviors occur). After this filter-training phase, the filter should detect (and reject) prompts that lead to harmful outputs. This is a natural approach which arises in practice. We ask:

\begin{center}
\textit{Can efficient input-detection filters always be created?}
\end{center}

We answer this question in the negative, showing a significant barrier to the natural approach of detection filtering.

The setup is as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item There is a computationally powerful LLM, which we do not trust to be aligned. In particular, there is a non-empty set of prompts that incite the LLM to produce harmful outputs, e.g., requesting instructions on how to build a bomb is considered harmful.

    \item We assume the existence of a very weak innocent prompt generator $G$: it generates prompts that (when fed into the LLM) do not produce harmful outputs. We further assume that the distribution of innocent prompts generated by $G$ is not low-entropy (i.e. there are many innocent prompts, see Remark~\ref{rem:G}).

    \item A prompt filter is a moderately powerful algorithm, which can be trained using real-world data and black-box access to the LLM but during deployment it does not have full access to the LLM architecture and weights. On getting a prompt from a user, the filter should reject it or pass it to the LLM. The goal is to reject prompts on which the LLM produces a harmful output. 

    The filter should be more efficient than the LLM: otherwise we can ignore the given LLM and just focus on training a new one from scratch and using it in place of a filter. 
    Additionally, if a filter was as powerful as the LLM, the question arises of how to align the filter itself.

    To avoid triviality (i.e. having a filter that rejects all prompts), we also require that the filter accepts prompts generated by the innocent prompt generator $G$ with high probability.

\end{enumerate}

Our main result shows the impossibility of prompt filtering:

\begin{theorem}[Prompt-detection filter impossibility, informal]
\label{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}
For any high-entropy innocent prompt generator \( G \),  there exists an adversarial prompt generator \( G' \) (with comparable runtime to \( G \)) and an LLM $M'$ such that prompts by $G'$ can induce $M'$ to produce harmful outputs, and under the assumption that cryptographic Time-Lock Puzzles exist, no prompt filter that runs significantly faster than $M'$ can distinguish between prompts generated by \( G \) and prompts generated by \( G' \).
\end{theorem}

