\paragraph{Discussion.}
A few remarks on our result are in order. 
First, our result relies on a computational asymmetry between the filter and the LLM. This is a realistic assumption: filtering is only meaningful when the LLM is too expensive or complex to retrain, and cannot be fully simulated by the filter.

Second, we show that there exist LLMs that cannot be aligned using prompt-filtering alone, even though the specific LLM we construct may exhibit contrived behavior. Nevertheless, the core message is more general: filtering harmful inputs without access to the LLM's internals is, in principle, impossible. 
In the current state of knowledge, where LLMs remain largely uninterpretable, this should serve as a warning against overconfidence in input-based safeguards—particularly when facing prompts carefully engineered to elicit harmful outputs.

Importantly, there is a long-standing precedent for such insights in cryptography. Theoretical vulnerabilities that initially appear artificial, overly stylized, or unlikely to occur in practice have repeatedly foreshadowed devastating real-world exploits. A striking example is the 2016 attack on Ethereum smart contracts \citep{smartcontracts}, in which a seemingly contrived re-entrancy vulnerability—once viewed as a theoretical edge case—was leveraged to steal over 60 million USD. 
Such incidents highlight a recurring pattern: foundational impossibility results, even when abstract or idealized, often uncover structural weaknesses that can have far-reaching consequences.

\begin{comment}
Additionally, recent work \citep{riskslearnedopt, alignmentfaking} demonstrates that qualitatively similar behaviors can emerge in practice. The phenomenon of \emph{deceptive alignment}, where a model appears aligned under training but behaves differently in deployment, illustrates that misalignment is not merely a theoretical concern. 
For instance, \citet{alignmentfaking} found that some LLMs conditionally comply with alignment objectives based on deployment context or user identity. While these empirical behaviors do not exactly replicate our construction, they reinforce the broader lesson: alignment failures that initially appear implausible or contrived can—and do—manifest in real systems.
\end{comment}

\paragraph{Construction.}
Our result relies on the existence of cryptographic \emph{Time-Lock Puzzles}. Introduced by \citet{rivest1996time} (RSW), time-lock puzzles provide a way to send messages ``to the future.'' A sender can \textbf{quickly} generate a puzzle whose solution~$s$ remains hidden until a \textbf{moderately large amount of computation}---quantified by a time parameter~$t$---has been performed after which it can be solved (or opened). 
The key property is that~$s$ remains inaccessible to any adversary running in significantly less time than~$t$.
\citet{rivest1996time} proposed a concrete construction based on the assumption that repeated squaring modulo a special RSA integer is an \emph{inherently sequential} task. The RSA integer, standing for Rivest, Shamir, and Adleman, is a product of two large primes, and appears in the context of public-key cryptography~\citep{rsa}.

Informally, in our setting, we use time-lock puzzles to conceal harmful instructions. An LLM that runs for time~$t$ can solve the puzzle and follow harmful instructions, while a filter incapable of spending that much time cannot even detect the existence of such instructions.


\paragraph{Further impossibility results.} We emphasize that the main result of this work is Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}. In addition, we show extensions to settings where the LLM actively collaborates with some malicious users (i.e. malicious prompt generators) to enable them to extract harmful outputs. Although such settings may be uncommon nowadays, these results are stronger than Theorem \ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock} in two senses: First, they require a weaker cryptographic assumption that the existence of time-lock puzzles. Second, they show impossibility for more powerful prompt filters, which can run in arbitrary polynomial time. The particular settings we consider are: (1) In Theorem \ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk}, the malicious prompt generator $G'$ shares secret randomness with the LLM (which is not known to the filter) and (2) in Theorem \ref{thm:detection-impossible-pk}, the prompt generator $G'$ depends on a public key associated with the LLM (which is also known to the filter). 

\begin{theorem}[Prompt-detection filter impossibility via secret keys, informal]
\label{thm:detection-impossible-sk}
%Assuming the existence of one-way functions, there exists an LLM and a super-efficient harmful prompt generator $G'$ that have a shared secret key where no \emph{ polynomial-time} detection filter (which doesn't know the secret key) can reject the prompts generated by $G'$: they are indistinguishable from innocent prompts.
For any high-entropy innocent prompt generator \( G \), there exists an adversarial prompt generator \( G' \) (with comparable runtime to \( G \)) and an LLM $M'$ that has a shared secret key with $G'$ such that $G'$ can induce $M'$ to produce harmful outputs and, under the assumption that One-Way Functions exist, no \textbf{polynomial-time} prompt filter can distinguish between prompts generated by \( G \) and prompts generated by \( G' \).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Prompt-detection filter impossibility via public keys, informal]
\label{thm:detection-impossible-pk}
There exists an LLM $M'$ with an associated public key, such that for any high-entropy innocent prompt generator \( G \), there exists an adversarial prompt generator $G'$ (with comparable runtime to \( G \) and which depends on the public key of $M'$) such that $G'$ can induce $M'$ to produce harmful outputs and, under the assumption that public key encryption exists, no \textbf{polynomial-time} prompt filter can distinguish between prompts generated by \( G \) and prompts generated by \( G' \).
%Assuming (trapdoor functions? permutations?), there exists an LLM with an associated public key, and a super-efficient harmful prompt generator $G'$ (that depends on the public key), where no {\em polynomial-time} detection filter that knows the public key can reject the prompts generated by $G'$: they are indistinguishable from innocent prompts.
\end{theorem}

