\paragraph{Deceptive Alignment.}
The LLMs constructed in Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock} behave in a contrived and deliberately pathological manner, and Theorems~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk} and~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-pk} rely on cooperation between LLMs and malicious users—scenarios that may at first glance seem overly pessimistic or implausible. However, recent empirical studies \citep{riskslearnedopt, alignmentfaking} have demonstrated that qualitatively similar behaviors can and do emerge in current-generation LLMs under realistic conditions.
The term \emph{deceptive alignment} refers to cases in which an AI system that is not genuinely aligned with human intent nonetheless behaves in an apparently aligned way, often strategically, in order to avoid detection, satisfy training objectives, or manipulate oversight. For example, \citet{alignmentfaking} observed that some LLMs modulate their behavior depending on whether they are in a training or deployment context, and on characteristics of the user they are interacting with—selectively choosing to comply with alignment objectives only in certain regimes.\footnote{In \citet{alignmentfaking}, the LLMs were misaligned during training and aligned during deployment, but one could easily imagine the reverse scenario.}
While these empirical findings do not exactly instantiate the theoretical constructions we present, they provide concrete evidence that the kind of strategic misalignment we model—once considered speculative—is already beginning to materialize in practice. This convergence between theory and empirical observation underscores the importance of understanding and anticipating even stylized forms of deceptive behavior.

\subsection{Output Filtering} 

We also consider an alternative approach to alignment: instead of filtering prompts, the defense mechanism filters the \emph{answers} produced by the LLM. We refer to this as an \emph{output filter}. In this setup, a prompt is given to the LLM, which generates a response; both the prompt and the response are then passed to the output filter, which may choose to detect and reject the output.
The goal of the output filter is twofold:  
(1) as with a prompt filter, it should reject harmful prompts; and  
(2) it should reject cases where \emph{the answer is harmful}, even if the prompt appears benign—e.g., when a seemingly innocent input results in a detailed recipe for constructing a bomb.

Output filters may be easier to design than prompt filters, since they operate with more information. 
This motivates the question:

\begin{center}
\textit{Can efficient output-detection filters always be created (even when prompt filters are impossible)?}
\end{center}

Again, we answer this question in the negative.


Note that if it is easy to determine whether a given LLM output is harmful, then output filters are trivial to construct. Thus, output filtering becomes meaningful only in the more challenging setting, where assessing the potential harm of a response is itself difficult. This concern becomes more pressing as AI systems grow more powerful, making it increasingly hard to evaluate the downstream consequences of their outputs. For example, imagine an AI generating a complex business plan aimed at reducing costs by subtly recommending the exclusion of clients with certain characteristics.


The setup for output-detection filtering is as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item There exists a harmfulness oracle that measures the level of harm of an answer.
    Neither of the parties has direct access to this oracle.
    %We assume that on the innocent prompts from $G$ the LLM answers correctly, i.e., with answers that are not harmful.
    \item An output filter is an algorithm that can be more computationally\footnote{As we explained for the prompt-detection filters case it is reasonable to assume that the filter is \emph{weaker} than the LLM. Otherwise, we can ignore the potentially malicious LLM. However, our results for the output-detection filters is stronger and holds also for filters computationally more powerful than the LLM.} powerful than the LLM, although it still runs in polynomial time (cannot break cryptography).
    It receives as input a prompt-answer pair and decides if it should be flagged as harmful.
\end{enumerate}

We show the following impossibility result:

\begin{theorem}[Output-detection filter impossibility, informal]\label{thm:detection-impossible-output}
For every LLM $M$ there exists an LLM $M'$ and a harmfulness oracle $\mathcal{H'}$ such that outputs of $M'$ are judged as harmful by $\mathcal{H'}$ and, under the assumption that Time-Lock Puzzles exist, no efficient output filter (even one that runs for more time than the LLM itself) can distinguish the outputs generated by $M'$ from outputs of $M$. Furthermore, $M'$ runtime is similar to the runtime of $M$.
\end{theorem}

This shows that there exist situations, in which a malicious LLM can generate outputs that are indistinguishable from safe responses but nevertheless have harmful consequences.

\subsection{Mitigation} 

Having established computational barriers for both prompt filters and output filters, we now examine a more permissive mechanism, which we term a \emph{prompt–mitigation filter}. 
Here, the filter can modify the prompt and pass it through to the LLM in addition to rejecting it. Note that innocent prompts can also be modified, but the implicit assumption is that the semantics of the prompts is maintained.
Mitigation gives the filter more power, which makes the goal of filtering or obtaining alignment potentially easier to achieve. We show a connection between the security of watermarking schemes and the impossibility of mitigation filtering.

\paragraph{Prompt mitigation and watermarking.}
A watermarking scheme \(W\) enables the creator of an LLM to prove that a given output originated from its model, even after an adversary has post-processed the text. Fully provable watermarking remains beyond the current state of the art: one typically demands that the adversary preserve \emph{some} semantic content—otherwise it could simply delete the text (and with it the watermark). We therefore consider watermarking against adversaries that apply edits from a permissible class \(E\) and run in time \(t\).  
The watermark should remain \emph{indistinguishable} to any such time-\(t\) adversary.

Our focus is on auto-regressive models, which generate text token by token, and on watermarking schemes that embed the mark by perturbing the model’s sampling randomness—a strategy explored by several recent proposals \citep{kirchenbauerwatermark,Kuditipudiwater,CGZ,golowichwatermark}.  

We show that:

\begin{theorem}[Impossibility of mitigation-filters, informal]
\label{thm:mitigation-impossible}
Let $W$ be a watermarking scheme as above that is resilient to edits from a class $E$.
For any high-entropy, innocent prompt generator \(G\), there exists an adversarial prompt generator $G'$ (with comparable runtime to \( G \)) and an LLM $M'$ such that 
$G'$ generates prompts that will induce harmful outputs from $M'$ even when $G'$'s outputs pass through an efficient prompt-mitigation filter using edits from the class $E$.
\end{theorem}

