\subsection{Intelligence, Judgment and Agency}

Our results can be interpreted as saying that it is impossible to achieve alignment by separating intelligence and judgment.
To illustrate this message we explain now how our paper relates to a recent proposal for achieving alignment.

\paragraph{Decoupling Intelligence and Agency as a path to AI alignment?} \cite{joshuaAIScientist} propose an approach to AI alignment by developing non-agentic “Scientist AIs”—systems that explain the world through Bayesian inference without taking actions or pursuing goals.
They argue that, unlike agentic AIs, which can become deceptive or self-preserving, Scientist AIs are designed to be interpretable, uncertainty-aware, and inherently trustworthy. 
The suggested applications of Scientist AIs are advancing scientific progress and monitoring agentic systems. 

\emph{Agency} is a complicated term to define. 
\cite{joshuaAIScientist} defines it as the ability to change the world to
achieve goals and mentions three pillars of agency: \emph{affordances, goal-directedness, and intelligence}. We refer the reader to Section~\ref{sec:reflections} for future directions for research related to agency and alignment.

\begin{comment}
Our results can be understood as pointing out limitations of such ideas.
Firstly, the authors suggest to use a Scientist AI A for generating candidate experiments and another Scientist AI B for judging whether they are safe.
Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-output} shows that even if B is more powerful than A, then it will not be able to filter out all dangerous experiments.
One might argue than a Scientist AI would not work in a way similar to the one in Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-output} as it is trained to be interpretable and trustworthy.
However, if this was the case then the need for an additional Scientist AI for monitoring is questionable?
\end{comment}

Our results point to limitations in the proposed use of Scientist AIs as safety guardrails.  
First, the authors suggest using a Scientist AI \( A \) to generate candidate experiments and another, more powerful Scientist AI \( B \) to assess their safety. However, Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-output} shows that even a stronger \( B \) may fail to detect all dangerous outputs.  
One might argue that a Scientist AI behaves differently from the LLM constructed in the proof of the theorem, being designed for interpretability and trustworthiness---but if that is true, the need for a second filtering AI is unclear.
Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-output} also indirectly \textbf{implies the need for agency}, as in some situations, there is no other way of judging the level of harm of an action than executing it in the world.

Second, if a Scientist AI is used to filter prompts for a more powerful agentic AI, Theorems~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock},~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk}, and~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-pk} show it cannot reliably block all harmful prompts. Section 3.1.5 of \citet{joshuaAIScientist} acknowledges that a guardrail AI must match the intelligence of the system it oversees. But if so, it is unclear why one would use the untrusted AI at all, given similar capabilities and likely similar computational cost of the Scientist AI.


\section{Technical Overview}
\label{sec:overview}

\paragraph{Preliminaries.} For $n \in \N$ we denote $\{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$ by $[n]$.\footnote{$[n]$ will be denoted by $\Z_n$ in the appendix.} A language model $M$ is a deterministic algorithm that takes as input a prompt $\mathsf{prompt}$ and a previous output by the model $z = (z_0,\dots,z_{i-1})$ and outputs a probability distribution over a token set $\mathcal{T}$. To sample a response of $M$ one repetitively samples from the probability distribution output by the model.

\paragraph{Construction.}
At a high level, all our results (Theorems~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock},~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk} and~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-pk}) on hardness of prompt detection filtering share the following proof structure:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Design a pair of algorithms $\mathsf{Samp}$ and $\mathsf{RecoverRand}$, where  $\mathsf{Samp}$ takes as input an auto-regressive model\footnote{We assume that the innocent prompt generator $G$ is a language model.} $G$ (see Appendix~\ref{apx:prelim} for a formal definition) and randomness $R$ and produces a prompt $z = (z_0,z_1, \dots)$; and  $\mathsf{RecoverRand}$ on input $z$ (and knowing $G$) recovers $R$. 
    The algorithms are formally defined in Algorithms~\ref{alg:samp} and~\ref{alg:rec}.
    \item Construct a function $g$ such that:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The LLM can compute $g$ on inputs of its choice.
        \item The adversarial prompt generator $G'$ can sample pairs $(x,g(x))$ where $x$ is (close to) uniformly random.
        \item The filter cannot distinguish between pairs $R = (x,\mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m))$ generated by $G'$ and pairs $(x,y)$ where $x$ and $y$ are  uniformly random and $y$ is  independent of $x$, for any $m$. We think of $m$ as a malicious prompt/message that is encrypted by an encryption algorithm $\mathsf{Enc}$ using a key $g(x)$.
        \item Given $g(x)$ and a ciphertext $c = \mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m)$, $m$ is easy to recover (using a decryption algorithm $\mathsf{Dec}$).
        %\item The output of $\mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m)$  is indistinguishable from random for all polynomial-time algorithms not knowing $g(x)$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

In the construction of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}, we design an adversarial prompt generator $G'$ and an LLM $M'$.
$G'$ works as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Sample $R \gets (x,\mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m))$, where $m$ is a prompt that can induce a harmful output from the LLM.
    \item Return $z \gets \mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$.
\end{enumerate}
$M'$ works as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Recover $R \gets \mathsf{RecoverRand}(G,z)$.
    \item Parse $R$ as $(x,\mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m))$ and compute $g(x)$.
    \item Recover $m$ from $c = \mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m)$, i.e., run the decryption algorithm $\mathsf{Dec}$ on $c$ using $g(x)$.
    \item \say{Execute}\footnote{We imagine there are many possible harmful modes that can be induced from $M'$. We model that by a collection of harmful prompts $m$ that can be \say{executed} by $M'$. Formally (see Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock-formal}) we say that for every model $M$ there exists $M'$ such that for every $m$, $m$ can be \say{hidden} in an innocent looking prompt (via $\mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m)$) such that $M'$ will recover and execute $m$.} the prompt $m$.
\end{enumerate}

It is easy to see that the filter cannot distinguish prompt $z$  from an innocent prompt sampled by $G$ , because $R$ is indistinguishable from random according to property (2c) above. 
However, $M'$ is able to recover the randomness, compute $g(x)$, recover $m$, and execute it.
%On the other hand, the LLM can run $\mathsf{RecoverRand}$ on the command $z$ to recover $R$, and by property (2a) then use its power to compute $g$ on $x$,  recover $m$ from $\mathsf{Enc}(g(x),m)$ and executes prompt $m$. 

The formal implementation of $G'$ is given in Algorithm~\ref{alg:harmincite} and the implementation of $M'$ is given in Algorithm~\ref{alg:maliciousllm}.
We proceed to show how to construct $g$ for the result of Theorem \ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}. 

\subsection{Construction of $g$}
\label{subsec:overview-g-construction}

%We focus on construction for Theorem \ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}, showing hardness of moderately efficient detection filters. 
\paragraph{Moderately hard $g$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock})}
Our construction is based on time-lock puzzles.
More concretely, we use the RSW time-lock that relies on the moderately hard repeated exponentiation function:
\begin{align*}
    f(n,r) = r^{(2^t)} \pmod{n},
\end{align*}
where $n$ is a product of two (random) primes $p$ and $q$, i.e., $n = p q$, $r$ is a random integer between $0$ and $n-1$ and $t$ is a fixed large integer. The assumption is that without knowing the factorization of $n$, computing $f$ takes time that scales linearly with $t$ (and moreover this computation cannot be parallelized). This function has several nice properties right off the bat. First, we can choose $t$ so that computing $f$ is feasible for the LLM (which can run computations that take more than $t$ steps), but not for a filter whose runtime is bounded to be significantly smaller than $t$. Further, it is easy to generate ``solved instances'': the adversarial prompt generator generates $n$, which is why it can know the primes $p$ and $q$. It can therefore compute $e = 2^t \pmod {\phi(n)}$ where we know that $r^e = r^{2^t} \pmod{n}$. Thus, the adversarial prompt generator only needs to compute a small number of exponentiations (only $\log(e)$).

Hence, $f$ seems like a natural fit to use for constructing the function $g$, but several issues arise. First, the composite $n$ is not a random string (which is what we need), rather it is a product of two large primes. 
However, we know that $n$ is uniformly random in the set of products of two primes $p$ and $q$ chosen at random from a set $[1,w]$. 
By the prime number theorem, the number of primes in that range is $\Theta(w / \log(w))$. 
Thus, $n$ is uniformly random in a domain of size  $S = \Theta((w / \log(w))^2)$. 
$G'$ hashes $n$ to a range of size $\Theta(\eps^{2} \cdot S)$ using a pairwise independent hash function $h$. 
By the leftover hash lemma the joint distribution of $(h,h(n))$ is $\eps$-close to uniformly random \citep{HastadILL99,Vadhan12}. 
It is straightforward to represent the random integer $r$ as a random bit string, so the entire input $x = (h,h(n),r)$ can be (close to) uniformly random, as we wanted. 
We remark that the LLM will need to recover $n$ from $(h,h(n))$: this is possible because the pairwise-independent hash function can be inverted efficiently (e.g., it can be a linear function, where inverses can be found using Gaussian elimination). 
Note however that there will be more than one inverse (because the hash function is shrinking), and this raises technical issues in the construction: the LLM will need to enumerate over all the possibilities. 
The final issue is that the output $r^{(2^t)} \pmod{n}$ is moderately hard for the filter to compute, but might not be indistinguishable from uniformly random (which is what we wanted). 
We handle this using standard techniques from the cryptography literature (namely using hardcore bits of $f$ \citep{GoldreichL89,Goldreich2001}). See the appendix for the full details of this construction.

\paragraph{Other time-lock puzzles.} 
Our construction is based on the RSW time-lock puzzle but we consider proving Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock} assuming general time-lock puzzles.
See Remark~\ref{rem:exttoTLP} in Appendix~\ref{apx:promptdetection} for details.

\paragraph{Secret key (Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk}) and public key (Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-pk}) constructions.} 
We focus on the impossibility results for moderately weak filters, i.e., weaker than the LLM in this overview. However, if we allow collaboration between the LLM and the prompt generator, it is possible to prove impossibility even against stronger filters. 
If we allow the LLM to publish a public key (and allow the adversarial prompt generator to depend on it) as in Theorem \ref{thm:detection-impossible-pk}, then we can use any trapdoor function to instantiate $\mathsf{Enc}$ function whose output will be pseudorandom to any polynomial-time filter that does not know the secret key. Here, we let the adversarial prompt generator $G'$ sample $R=\mathsf{Enc}(\mathsf{pk},m)$, where $\mathsf{pk}$ is a public key, and returns the prompt $z \gets \mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$. 
The LLM recovers $R$ from $z$ and uses its matching secret key $\mathsf{sk}$ to recover $m$ from $R$. 
If we allow the LLM and the adversarial prompt generator to have shared randomness (or rather shared secret key $\mathsf{sk}$) as in Theorem \ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk}, then we can construct $G'$ without making any assumptions: we use the shared randomness as a one-time pad to create a single special prompt that leads to a harmful output. Namely, $G'$  sets $R = \mathsf{sk} \oplus m$ for a prompt $m$ which can induce a harmful output from the LLM and returns $z \gets \mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$.  Alternatively, under the (fairly relaxed) cryptographic assumption that one-way functions exist, even a short shared random seed can be used to generate an \emph{arbitrary (polynomial) number} of harmful prompts that are indistinguishable from innocent ones.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{\(\mathsf{Samp}(G, R)\) \Comment{\(G\): a prompt generator; \(R \in \{0,1\}^*\): the randomness}}\label{alg:samp}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \(i \gets 0\), \(j \gets 0, q \gets (), k \gets 1 \)
\While{\(\mathsf{done} \not\in (z_0, \dots, z_{i-1})\)}
    \State \(p_i \gets G(z_0, \dots, z_{i-1})\)
    \State \(d \gets  \argmin_{d' \in \N} [k \cdot 2^{d'} \geq 2^P] \) \Comment{\(P\) is a precision parameter to be set later}
    %\State \(k \gets 2^{\ceil{\log(2^t \cdot k)}}\)
    \State \(k \gets 2^d \cdot k \)
    \State \(q \gets q \ \| \ R[j: j+d]\) \Comment{$\|$ is a concatenation}
    \State \(j \gets j+d\)

    \If{\(q/k < p_i\)}
        \State \(z_i \gets 1\)
        \State \(k \gets \ceil{p_i \cdot k}\)
    \Else
        \State \(z_i \gets 0\)
        \State \(q \gets q - \floor{p_i \cdot k} \)
        \State \(k \gets k - \floor{p_i \cdot k} \)
    \EndIf
    \State \(i \gets i + 1\)
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{\(\mathsf{RecoverRand}(G, z, L)\) \Comment{\(G\): a prompt generator; \(z \in \{0,1\}^*\); \(L\): a length of the randomness required}}\label{alg:rec}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \(j \gets 0, a \gets 0, b \gets 1, k \gets 1 \)
\For{\(i \in [\mathsf{len}(z)]\)}
    \State \(p_i \gets G(z_0, \dots, z_{i-1})\)
    \State \(d \gets  \argmin_{d' \in \N} [k \cdot 2^{d'} \geq 2^P] \) \Comment{\(P\) is a precision parameter to be set later}
    \State \(k \gets 2^d \cdot k \)
    \State \(a \gets a \ \| \ 0^t, b \gets b \ \| \ 1^t\) \Comment{$\|$ is a concatenation}
    %\State \(q \gets q \ \| \ R[j: j+t]\)
    %\State \(j \gets j+t\)
    \State \(\mathsf{mid} \gets a + (b-a) \cdot p_i/k\)
    \If{\(z_i = 1\)}
        \State \(a \gets \mathsf{mid}\)
        %\State \(b \gets a + (b-a) \cdot p_i/k\)
        %\State \(R \gets R \ \| \ \mathsf{CommonPrefix}(a,b)\)
        \State \(k \gets \ceil{p_i \cdot k}\)
    \Else
        %\State \(a \gets a + (b-a) \cdot p_i/k\)
        \State \(a \gets \mathsf{mid}\)
        %\State \(R \gets R \ \| \ \mathsf{CommonPrefix}(a,b)\)
        \State \(k \gets k - \floor{p_i \cdot k} \)
    \EndIf
    \If{\(\mathsf{len}(\mathsf{CommonPrefix}(a,b)) \geq L\)}
        \Return \(\mathsf{CommonPrefix}(a,b)\)
    \EndIf
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Recoverable-Randomness Sampling}
\label{sec:overview-recoverable-randomness
}
In our construction, it is key that the distribution of $G'$ is indistinguishable from $G$ and that it is possible to effectively recover the randomness used by $G'$ from its outputs.
To achieve this, we design an algorithm $\mathsf{Samp}$ (Algorithm~\ref{alg:samp}) that takes as input a generator $G$ and randomness $R$ and produces $z = (z_0,z_1, \dots)$; and an algorithm $\mathsf{RecoverRand}$ (Algorithm~\ref{alg:rec}) that given $z$ and $G$ recovers $R$.
We assume that the set of tokens over which $G$ operates equals $\{0,1\}$.
This is possible because of a simple reduction (see Appendix~\ref{apx:prelim}).

The high-level idea of our scheme is to maintain \say{residual randomness}.
Imagine, that at some point in the generation process, $G(z_0, \dots, z_{i-1}) \rightarrow 1/4$.
Then to generate $z_i$ accurately $2$ bits of randomness are needed, i.e., if the bits are $00$ we set $z_i = 1$ and we set $z_i = 0$ if the bits are $00,01,11$.
If $z_i = 0$ then the recovering algorithm might not know which of the three options for the randomness determined $z_i$.
To address this issue we \say{reuse} this randomness in the generation of the next tokens.
More concretely, if $z_i = 0$ then we have $\log_2(3)$ bits of residual randomness that we can use in generating $z_{i+1},z_{i+2},\dots$.
If done properly this \say{reusing} process will allow perfect recovery of the randomness used.
Intuitively, because all of the randomnness will be used.

Our scheme, defined in Algorithms~\ref{alg:samp} and~\ref{alg:rec}, works as follows.
At all times, the sampling algorithm $\mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$ maintains a precision range $k$ and a value $q \in [k]$.
The value $q$ is created on the fly using the randomness $R$ and will determine how tokens are sampled.
Intuitively, $q$ maintains the \say{residual randomness}.
$\mathsf{Samp}$ builds a response $(z_0,\dots,z_{i-1})$ iteratively, where in the $i$-th step it: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Calls $p_i \gets G(z_0, \dots, z_{i-1})$.
    \item If $k < 2^P$ it multiplies $k$ by an appropriate power $2^t$ so that $k \geq 2^P$,\footnote{$P$ is a precision parameter that will determine the closeness of the generated distribution to the distribution of $G$.} and increases the precision of $q$ by concatenating it with the not yet used randomness from $R$, i.e., \(q \gets q \ \| \ R[j: j+t]\). 
    This ensures that $q$ represents an element of $[k]$.
    \item If \(q/k < p_i\) it sets $z_i \gets 1$ and sets $z_i \gets 0$ otherwise.
    Additionally, it updates $q$ and $k$ so that the \say{residual randomness} can be used later on.
    Intuitively, if $q/k < p_i$ then we want to keep the $\approx \log_2(p_i \cdot k)$ bits of randomness.
    To do that we update $k \gets k - \floor{p_i \cdot k}$, which implicitly represents that $q$ is now a random value from $[k - \floor{p_i \cdot k}]$.
    If $q/k > p_i$ the values are updated accordingly.
\end{enumerate}
To recover the randomness $\mathsf{RecoverRand}(G,z)$ works as follows.
It maintains bitstrings $a,b$ that informally are \say{lower and upper-bounds} on the randomness $R$ being recovered. 
More concretely, in the first step $\mathsf{RecoverRand}$ knows that the prefix of randomness $R$ is between $0^P$ and $1^P$.
If $p_0 \gets G()$ and $z_0 = 1$ then it knows that the prefix of $R$ is between $a = 0^P$ and $b = p_0 \cdot 2^P$ written in binary on $P$ bits.
By construction, the common prefix of $a,b$ is equal to the prefix of $R$ - this will allow to recover $R$.
What is left to show is that it is possible to maintain $a$ and $b$ correctly.
Observe that it is possible to update $k$ exactly like $\mathsf{Samp}$ does it because $k$ changes depending only on the value of $z_i$.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{\(\mathsf{HarmIncite}(G,m,t)\) \Comment{$G$: innocent prompt generator; $m$: harmful prompt; $t$: time parameter}}\label{alg:harmincite}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Sample two large primes \(p,q\)
\State Set $n \gets p \cdot q$
\State Compute $e \gets 2^t \pmod{(p-1)(q-1)}$ \Comment{$\phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1)$}
\State Sample $r \gets [n]$
\State Compute $s \gets r^e \pmod{n}$
%\State \(((N, r), s) \gets \mathsf{Gen}(t,1^\secp)\)
\State Sample \(h\) a pairwise independent hash function
\State \(c \gets \mathsf{Enc}(s,m)\) \Comment{For $\mathsf{Enc}$ defined in the Appendix~\ref{apx:timelock}}
\State \(R \gets h \ \| \ h(n) \ \| \ r \ \| \ c\) \Comment{Concatenate components}
\State \Return \(z \gets \mathsf{Samp}(G, R)\) \Comment{Sample output so that randomness is recoverable}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{\(\mathsf{MaliciousLLM}(M, z, G, t)\) \Comment{\(M\): LLM; \(z\): input; \(G\): weak innocent prompt generator; $t$: time parameter}}\label{alg:maliciousllm}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \(R \gets \mathsf{RecoverRand}(G, z)\)
\State Parse \(R\) as \(h \ \| \ u \ \| \ r \ \| \ c\)
\ForAll{\(\hat{n} \in h^{-1}(u)\)}
    \State \(\hat{s} \gets r\)
    \For{\(i \in [t]\)}
        \State \(\hat{s} \gets \hat{s}^2 \pmod{\hat{n}}\)
    \EndFor
    \State \(m \gets \mathsf{Dec}(\hat{s}, c)\) \Comment{$m$ is the malicious prompt extracted from ciphertext $c$}
    \If{\(m \neq \bot\)} \Comment{$\mathsf{Dec}$ can detect if the decryption was successful}
        \State \Return \(M(m)\)
    \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return \(M(z)\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Output Filtering}

\paragraph{Setup.}
Before elaborating on the construction, we describe the setup in more detail.  
A \emph{harmfulness oracle} is a function \(\mathcal{H} : \mathcal{T}^* \rightarrow [0, \infty)\).  
Given an output \(z \in \mathcal{T}^*\) of a language model, \(\mathcal{H}(z)\) represents its harmfulness: \(\mathcal{H}(z) = 0\) indicates that \(z\) is benign, while higher values denote greater harmfulness.
During inference, neither the LLM nor the filter has query access to \(\mathcal{H}\).  
The oracle \(\mathcal{H}\) can be interpreted as the environment or nature ``evaluating'' the harmfulness of \(z\).  
However, the filter (and the LLM) may have received training examples of both harmful and benign outputs, or an approximate description of \(\mathcal{H}\), during training.

\paragraph{Construction.}
We leverage techniques used in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock} to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-output}.
Let $M$ be an LLM that doesn't produce harmful outputs.
%i.e., for prompts coming from some distribution $\dist$, if $z \gets \dist$, $y \gets M(z)$ then $\mathcal{H}_1(y) = 0$, where $\mathcal{H}_1$ outputs $0$ if $y$ is not harmful.

To define $M'$ we apply the same process as the one used to transform $G \dashrightarrow G'$ but applied to $M$.
When we apply the transformation, we set $t$ of the RSW time-lock to be exponential in the time complexity of $M$.
This ensures that the outcome of the transformation, i.e.,  $M'$, is such that its outputs are indistinguishable from outputs of $M$ for all polynomial, in the running time of $M$, filters.

To define $\mathcal{H}'$ we apply the same process as the one used to transform the LLM in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}.
More concretely, $\mathcal{H}'(z)$ \say{runs}\footnote{$\mathcal{H}'$ is a harmfulness oracle and not an algorithm, so formally it does not run.} as follows: it recovers the randomness used by $M'$, i.e., $R \gets \mathsf{RecoverRand}(M',z)$.
If $R$ contains a Time-Lock Puzzle, $\mathcal{H}'$ solves it to obtain an output $m$ and \say{declares} that $z$ is harmful (with harmfulness level determined by $m$).

The sketch of why this construction is correct is as follows.
Note that similarly as for $G$ and $G'$ in the case of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}, $M'$ outputs are indistinguishable from the outputs of $M$ for all polynomial-time filters.
Moreover, $M'$ on every harmful $m$, \say{embeds} it in its output $z$, $\mathcal{H}'$ \say{recovers} $m$ from $z$ and \say{declares} that $z$ is harmful with the harmfulness level determined by $m$.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}
\textbf{Alignment.} Making AI models aligned with human preferences is a central concern of contemporary AI research \citep{amodei2016concrete, leike2018scalable, hendrycks2021unsolved, ji2023ai}. In order to guide model behavior, technical efforts have focused on techniques like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF, \citealp{ christiano2017deep}), further improved by Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) \citep{rafailov2023direct}. However, a growing body of work suggests that achieving robust alignment is profoundly difficult: Researchers have highlighted issues ranging from the inherent ambiguity in specifying human preferences \citep{gabriel2020artificial, zhi2024beyond, sorensen2024roadmap}, to problems like shallow alignment induced by properties of the alignment algorithms \citep{jain2023mechanistically, kotha2023understanding, lee2024mechanistic} and the alignment data \citep{qi2024safety}. For instance, \citet{lee2024mechanistic} show that the DPO alignment algorithm only teaches a model to bypass toxic ``regions’’ without removing toxic knowledge gained from the pre-training stage, making it possible to elicit this toxic knowledge with ``jailbreaks’’---changes to the prompt that lead a model from answer-refusal to generating harmful information. Relatedly, \citet{qi2024safety} argue that safety alignment often takes shortcuts by primarily adapting a model's generative distribution over just its first few output tokens, making them vulnerable to various attacks. The difficulty in robustly aligning models at a deep representational level underscores the need for complementary external mechanisms to detect or prevent harmful model outputs. This is in line with regulatory frameworks such as the EU AI Act, which requires AI systems in the high-risk category to implement an effective risk management system (see Article 9 \citealp{euai}). 

\textbf{Filters.} In response to the need for safer AI systems, practical filtering mechanisms
%, sometimes called AI control \citep{greenbaltAIcontrol}, 
have been developed and deployed. 
For instance, model developers like Meta have introduced tools such as Llama Guard, designed to classify content as safe or unsafe \citep{inan2023llama}. Similarly, cloud service providers like Microsoft Azure offer content filtering capabilities within their AI service implementations \citep{microsoft_azure_ai_content_safety}, and companies like Nvidia also provide solutions aimed at moderating AI-generated content \citep{nvidia_nemo_guardrails}. These approaches represent an ongoing evolution, with classifiers and filters becoming increasingly sophisticated. However, the development of jailbreaks poses a consistent challenge to successful filtering and model alignment in general \citep{andriushchenko2024jailbreaking, chao2024jailbreakbench, xu2024comprehensive}. Against the background of this dynamic co-evolution of attack and defense, our work explores the computational intractability of  filtering approaches under cryptographic assumptions. 

\textbf{Time Lock Puzzles.}
It is usually desired that cryptographic schemes cannot be broken by any adversary. An exception is the notion of cryptographic puzzles that can be solved in some preset amount of time (or space) but not faster. Examples of such puzzles \citep{DN92, rivest1996time} have been used as a way to combat spam or send messages into the future, forcing the spammer (or the future reader of messages) to invest the preset amount of time. The notion of time-lock puzzles introduced by \citet{rivest1996time} following May's time-released cryptography \citep{May93} is especially intriguing in that it allows a user to quickly encrypt a message in such a way that it can be read only after a longer but set number of time steps. Informally, the sender generates a puzzle with
a solution $s$ that remains hidden from adversaries that run in time significantly less than $t$, including parallel adversaries with polynomially many processors. The original \citep{rivest1996time} candidate was based on the assumption that exponentiation modulo an RSA integer is an ``inherently sequential'' computation. More recently \citet{BGJPVW16} constructed time-lock puzzles based on the necessary assumption that worst-case Non-Parallelizing Languages exist-decidable in time $t$, but hard in the worst case for circuits of depth significantly smaller than $t$-and that indistinguishability obfuscation exists \citep{BGL+15, CHJV15}.
A culmination of a series of follow-up works \citep{giulioTLP, bitanskyTLP, giulioTLPfinal} managed to construct time-lock puzzles based on a version of the Learning With Errors \citep{regevLWE} (LWE) assumption. 
One advantage of basing time-lock puzzles on LWE is that the other constructions are broken by quantum algorithms, while LWE is a candidate for post-quantum secure cryptography.


