\subsection{Time-Lock Puzzles}

Time-lock puzzles, introduced by \citet{rivest1996time}, provide a mechanism for sending messages ``to the future''. The core idea is to enable a sender to quickly generate a cryptographic puzzle whose solution is guaranteed to remain hidden until a specified amount of computational time has elapsed. 
Additionally, this security must hold even against highly parallel adversaries equipped with polynomially many processors. 
The following is a definition of a Time-Lock Puzzle adapted from \cite{shafitimelocks}.

\begin{definition}[Time-Lock Puzzle]\label{def:tlp}
A \emph{Time-Lock Puzzle} (TLP) is defined over a difficulty parameter $t \in \mathbb{N}$ and a security parameter $\secp$. 
A Time-Lock Puzzle consists of a pair of algorithms $\mathsf{Puzzle} = (\mathsf{Gen}, \mathsf{Sol})$ with the following properties:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Puzzle Generation:}
    \[
    Z \gets \mathsf{Gen}(t, s)
    \]
    is a probabilistic algorithm that takes as input a difficulty parameter $t$ and a solution $s \in \{0,1\}^\lambda$ and outputs a puzzle $Z$.
    \item \textbf{Puzzle Solving:}
    \[
    s \gets \mathsf{Sol}(Z)
    \]
    is a deterministic algorithm that takes a puzzle $Z$ and returns a solution $s$.

    \item \textbf{Completeness:} For all difficulty parameters $t \in \mathbb{N}$ and $s \in \{0,1\}^\secp$:
    \[
    \mathsf{Sol}(\mathsf{Gen}(t,s)) = s.
    \]

    \item \textbf{Efficiency:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\mathsf{Gen}(t, s)$ runs in time $\mathsf{poly}(\log t, \lambda)$.
        \item $\mathsf{Sol}(Z)$ runs in time $t \cdot \mathsf{poly}(\lambda)$.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Sequentiality (Time-Lock Property):} 
    %There exists an $\e < 1$ and a polynomial $\underline{t}$ such that for all circuits of depth $t^\e$,on input $Z$, the probability of distinguishing between the case of $Z$ generated by $\mathsf{Gen}(t,s_0)$ or $\mathsf{Gen}(t,s_1)$ (for any two $s_0,s_1$) is negligible. 
    There exists $\varepsilon < 1$ and a polynomial $\underline{t}(\cdot)$, such that for every polynomial $t(\cdot) \geq \underline{t}(\cdot)$ and every polysize adversary $\mathcal{A} = \{\mathcal{A}_\lambda\}_{\lambda \in \mathbb{N}}$ of depth $\mathrm{dep}(\mathcal{A}_\lambda) \leq t^{\varepsilon}(\lambda)$, there exists a negligible function $\mu$, such that for every $\lambda \in \N$, and every pair of solutions $s_0, s_1 \in \{0,1\}^\lambda$:
        \[
        \Pr\Big[
        b \leftarrow \mathcal{A}_\lambda(Z) : b \leftarrow \{0,1\},
        Z \leftarrow \mathsf{Gen}(t(\lambda), s_b)
        \Big]
        \leq \frac{1}{2} + \mu(\lambda).
        \]
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

