\subsubsection{Motivation and Requirements}

Suppose a sender wishes to encrypt a message $m$ such that it will be revealed only after roughly $t$ computational steps, where $t$ is moderately large (e.g., several minutes or hours). A valid time-lock puzzle should satisfy the following properties:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Efficient Generation:} The puzzle and its solution can be generated in time polynomial in the security parameter.
    \item \textbf{Sequential Extraction:} Solving the puzzle requires $\Theta(t)$ sequential steps, even for an adversary with massive parallelism.
\end{itemize}
\end{comment}

\subsubsection{The RSW Construction}

\citet{rivest1996time} proposed a candidate construction based on the conjectured sequential nature of modular exponentiation. Their scheme uses the fact that repeated squaring modulo an RSA integer is believed to be inherently sequential.

$\mathsf{Gen}(t)$ proceeds as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Samples two primes $p, q$ from $[1,w]$, for a parameter $w$, and set $n = pq$.
    \item Samples $r \gets \mathbb{Z}_n^*$, where $\Z_n^* = \{1,2,\dots, n-1\}$, and computes
    \[
        s \gets r^e \bmod n,
    \]
    where $e = 2^{t}$ is a tower of repeated squarings (i.e., $t$ squarings of $r$).
    To compute $r^e \bmod n$ it first evaluates $\phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1)$ and reduces the exponent $e' \gets e \bmod \phi(n)$.
    Then $s = r^{e'} \bmod n$, which can be computed in only $O(\log(e'))$ modular exponentiations.
    \item Defines $Z = (n, r)$ to be the puzzle and $s$ its solution. Returns $(Z,s)$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{note}
$\mathsf{Gen}$ can be adapted to accept an additional input $s$ so that it adheres to the schema from Definition~\ref{def:tlp}. 
To do that, one would use a $\mathsf{root}$ function (Definition~\ref{def:root}) that for $x \in \Z_n^*$ computes \say{$\sqrt{x}$} so that it is possible to compute $r$ from $s$. However, there are some technical details that need to be addressed as $\mathsf{root}$ is well defined only on a subgroup of $\Z_n^*$ (see Fact~\ref{fact:residues}). We implicitly address these issues in Lemma~\ref{lem:hardcore}. We chose to use this formulation of the RSW construction for simplicity of exposition.    
\end{note}

$\mathsf{Sol}(Z)$ works as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Parses $Z = (n,r)$.
    \item Returns $r^{2^t}$.
\end{enumerate}

Note that $\mathsf{Sol}$ computes $s$ using $t$ repeated squarings, each of which is inexpensive, but the full process requires $\Theta(t)$ sequential steps.

