\subsubsection{Correctness and Efficiency}

Any party holding the factorization of $N$ (i.e., $p$ and $q$) can compute $\phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1)$ and thus reduce the exponent modulo $\phi(N)$:
\[
e' := e \bmod \phi(N).
\]
Then $R$ can be recovered directly as $r^{e'} \bmod n$ in a single modular exponentiation. This enables the holder of the factorization of $n$ to retrieve $s$ quickly.

An honest party without the factorization must compute $R$ using $t$ repeated squarings, each of which is inexpensive, but the full process requires $\Theta(t)$ sequential steps. Once $R$ is computed, decryption of $c$ yields $m$.
\end{comment}

\paragraph{Sequentiality.} The security of the RSW puzzle hinges on the assumption that modular exponentiation---specifically, computing $r^{2^t} \bmod n$---cannot be substantially accelerated via parallelism. That is any adversary who does not know the factorization of $n$ must essentially compute each squaring step sequentially.

This assumption is supported by decades of experience in cryptographic implementation and is believed to be secure. 
Although no formal proof of sequentiality is known, the construction remains a compelling candidate and has served as the basis for more advanced cryptographic primitives, such as time-release encryption \citep{bonehVDF, bonehtimedcommitments}.

\subsection{Public key encryption}
\citet{diffie1976} introduced the notion of public key encryption (PKE), enabling secure communication between parties without a shared secret. In a PKE scheme, each user generates a pair of keys: a public key $\mathsf{pk}$ that is distributed openly and a private key $\mathsf{sk}$ that is kept secret. Anyone can encrypt a message $m$ using $\mathsf{pk}$ to obtain a ciphertext $c$, but only the holder of $\mathsf{sk}$ can decrypt $c$ to recover $m$. Security relies on the assumed hardness of inverting the encryption without access to the secret key---for example, the difficulty of factoring large integers in the RSA scheme.

\subsection{Secret key encryption}
Secret key encryption (also known as symmetric-key encryption) requires both parties to share a common secret key $\mathsf{k}$ in advance. The encryption algorithm uses $\mathsf{k}$ to map a message $m$ to a ciphertext $c$, and the decryption algorithm uses the same key $\mathsf{k}$ to recover $m$ from $c$. Symmetric schemes are typically much more efficient than public key schemes and form the backbone of practical secure communication when a secure channel for key exchange is already available. Notable constructions include block ciphers such as the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) \citep{aes} and stream ciphers built from pseudorandom generators.

\section{Recoverable-Randomness Sampling}

\begin{definition}
For $\alpha : \N \rightarrow \N$ we say that a pair of algorithms $(\mathsf{Samp},\mathsf{RecoverRand})$ is an $\alpha$-Recoverable-Randomness Sampling (RRS) scheme if
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathsf{Samp}(G) \rightarrow y$, given a language model $G$\footnote{See Remark~\ref{rem:G} for why $G$ is a language model.} over token set $\mathcal{T}$, $\mathsf{Samp}$ samples an output $z \in \mathcal{T}^*$.
    \item $\mathsf{RecoverRand}(G,z,L) \rightarrow R$, for $y \in \mathcal{T}^*$, a language model $G$, $\mathsf{RecoverRand}$, and $L \in \N$ is a deterministic algorithm that recovers $L$ bits of the randomness used by $\mathsf{Samp}(G)$ when generating $z$.
\end{itemize}
The pair $(\mathsf{Samp},\mathsf{RecoverRand})$ satisfies
\begin{itemize}
    \item{\textbf{Recoverability.}} For every $R \in \{0,1\}^*$ and every $L \in \N, L \leq \mathsf{len}(R)$ if $\mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$ halts and returns $z_R = \mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$ then
    \[
    \mathsf{RecoverRand}(G,z_R,L) = R[0:L],
    \]
    In the notation $\mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$, $R$ explicitly specifies the randomness used.
    \item{\textbf{Closeness of Distributions.}}
    Distirbutions over $\mathcal{T}^*$ given by $\mathsf{Samp}(G)$ and $G$ are statistically close.
    Formally, for every $L \in \N$, 
    \[
    \frac12 \sum_{z \in \mathcal{T}^*, |z| \leq L} \Big| \Prob_R[\mathsf{Samp}(G,R) = z] - \Prob[G = z] \Big| \leq \alpha(L).
    \]
    Note that the expression on the left-hand side evaluates a partial statistical distance.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{rem:G}
In Theorems~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock},~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk} and~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-pk} model $G$ is a generator of innocent prompts.
The theorems hold for \emph{any} high-entropy model.    

We assume that $M$ (see for instance Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}) is deployed on some distribution $\dist$, e.g., a distribution of a user prompts for a chatbot.
We think of $G$ as a model generating prompts from $\dist$ or approximation thereof.
It is natural to assume such a $G$ exists.
In the example above, the top chatbots are used by millions of users, so finding a single $G$ whose outputs are indistinguishable from some users' prompts should be easy.
\end{remark}

