$P$ will be set to an exact value when $\mathsf{Samp}$ will be used as a subroutine in the proof of a formal version of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock}.
It is also informative to point out why the two distributions can be different in the first place.
The main reason is that $\mathsf{Samp}$ samples $z_i = 1$ with probability $\approx p_i$ (and not $= p_i$).
It is because we didn't assume anything about the precision of $p_i$ and it might \say{fall} in between the grid points defined by $q/k$, where $q \in [k]$.
