\subsection{Proof of correctness}

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:recoveringworks}
Algorithms~\ref{alg:samp} and~\ref{alg:rec} constitute an $O(L \cdot 2^L \cdot 2^{-P})$-Recoverable-Randomness Sampling scheme.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The result follows from a series of facts.

$\mathsf{Samp}$ satisfies the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $q \in [k]$ at all times.
    \item for every iteration $i$:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item for every $z_0,\dots,z_{i-1}$, before the if statement (step 8 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:samp}) is executed, the distribution of $q$ is uniform in $[k]$.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}
Observe that if these properties hold, then $z_i$'s are sampled from a distribution that is close to $G$.
The only reason the distributions might be different is the aforementioned precision issue.
However, step 4 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:samp} ensures that $|q/k-p_i| \leq 2^{-P}$, so the errors accumulate as required, as for every $(z_0,\dots,z_{L-1})$ the difference between sampling probabilities is upper bounded by $O(L \cdot 2^{-P})$.

The above properties are proven by induction over $i$.
%The updates inside the if statement ensure that the above properties are satisfied.
Because $R$ is a random bit-string it implies that $q$ is uniform in $[0,\ceil{p_i \cdot k}]$ conditioned on the event $q/k < p_i$.
This implies that if we update $k \gets \ceil{p_i \cdot k}$ then the property that $q$ is uniform in the updated $[k]$ is satisfied.

The properties of the $\mathsf{RecoverRand}$ algorithm are as follows.
For every iteration $i$: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $k$ and $d$ at the beginning of the iteration are equal to $k$ and $d$ in the $\mathsf{Samp}$ algorithm at the beginning of iteration $i$.
    \item the lengths of $a,b$ and $q$ from $\mathsf{Samp}$ are equal before the if statement (step 8 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:rec}).
    \item $a \leq_\text{lex} q \leq_\text{lex} b$, where $\leq_\text{lex}$ is the lexicographic order, before the if statement (step 8 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:rec}).
\end{enumerate}
Similarly, as for $\mathsf{Samp}$ these properties can be proven by induction over $i$.

The first property follows from the observation that $k$ and $d$ are updated depending only on the $z_i$ values.
The second property follows from the first property.
The third property is the crucial one and follows from the previous two and a careful analysis of how $a,b$ are updated in the if statement.
More concretely, note that $a + (b-a)\cdot p_i/k$ is the \say{threshold randomness} at which $\mathsf{Samp}$ would generate $z_i = 0$ vs $z_i = 1$.
Indeed, if $R \in [a,a + (b-a)\cdot p_i/k)$ then conditioned on $\mathsf{Samp}$ generating $z_0,\dots,z_{i-1}$, $\mathsf{Samp}$ would set $z_i \gets 1$.
If $R \in [a + (b-a)\cdot p_i/k),b)$ then it would generate $z_i = 0$.
\end{proof}

