\subsection{Shared secret key}

The following is a formal version of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-sk}.

\begin{theorem}[Impossibility of prompt-detection filters via secret keys]
\label{thm:detection-impossible-secret key-formal}
There exists a polynomial $p$ such that for all security parameters $\secp \in \N$, every set of harmful prompts $S_H \subseteq \{0,1\}^{l_m}$, where $l_m = \Theta(p(\secp)), \Omega(p(\secp)) = \log(|S_H|) \leq l_m - \Omega(p(\secp))$, for every language model $M$, for every %sequence of prompt generators $G = \{G_\secp\}_{\secp \in \N}$ satisfying $H(G_\secp) \geq \poly(\secp, \log(t))$ 
prompt generator $G$ satisfying $H(G) \geq \poly(\secp)$ and running in time $\poly(\secp)$ per token the following conditions hold.

There exists an adversarial prompt generator $G'$ with a running time $\poly(\secp)$ per token and a language model $M'$ with a running time $\poly(\secp) + O(T_M)$ per token, where $T_M$ is the running time of $M$ per token, such that if $M'$ and $G'$ share an $\poly(\secp)$-bit secret key then the following conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item{\textbf{($G'$ incites harmful behavior in $M'$)}.} For every malicious prompt $m \in S_H$, the following two distributions are close:
    \[
    \left\{M'(z) \ \middle| \ z \gets G'(m) \right\}, \left\{M(m) \right\}.
    \]
    In words, for every harmful prompt from $S_H$ the outputs of $M'$ and $M$ follow almost the same distribution, in particular, a similar harmful behavior is induced for $M$ and $M'$.
    \item{\textbf{($M'$ and $M$ behave identically on $G$ almost always)}.} With all but negligible in $\secp$ probability over $z \gets G$ the distributions $M'(z)$ and $M(z)$ are equal.
    \item{\textbf{($G$ and $G'$ are indistinguishable for filters running in polynomial-time)}.} Under the assumption that One-Way Functions exist, for every prompt filter running in polynomial-time, for every $m \in S_H$ the advantage of the filter in distinguishing outputs of $G$ and $G'(m)$ is negligible in $\secp$.
    In words, no filter running in polynomial-time can reject\footnote{If it doesn't also reject the innocent prompts.} the adversarial prompts from $G'$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\subsubsection{Construction}

As we discussed in Section~\ref{sec:overview}, instead of using time-locks, we use symmetric key (shared secret key) encryption (it is known that one-way functions (OWFs) imply symmetric key encryption).
Namely, for $m \in S_H$ we set $R := \mathsf{Enc}(\mathsf{sk},m)$, where $\mathsf{Enc}$ is the symmetric key encryption scheme, and $\mathsf{sk}$ is the shared secret key.
Next, $G'$ generates $z \gets \mathsf{Samp}(G,R)$ as before.
$M'$ decrypts the randomness $R$ to get $\hat{m}$ and checks if $\hat{m}$ belongs to the set $S_H$, and if yes returns $M(\hat{m})$. 

The proof of correctness is a simplified version of the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock-formal}.

