\section{Impossibility of Output-Detection Filters}

\paragraph{Preliminaries.}
A harmfulness oracle is a function $\mathcal{H} : \mathcal{T}^* \rightarrow [0,\infty)$.
For an output of a language model $z \in \mathcal{T}^*$ we interpret $\mathcal{H}(z)$ as the harmfulness of $z$, where $\mathcal{H}(z) = 0$ means that $z$ is benign and a high-value denotes high harmfulness.\footnote{It is important not to confuse the notation for harmfulness oracle $\mathcal{H}$ with the notation for entropy $H$.} 

The following is a formal version of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-output}.

\begin{theorem}[Impossibility of output-detection filters]
\label{thm:detection-impossible-output-formal}
There exists $\e \in (0,1)$, polynomial $p$, such that for all security parameters $\secp \in \N$, 
%every distribution $D$,\footnote{We think of $D$ as a distribution of \say{normal} prompts on which $M$ performs well, i.e., such that it returns outputs judged as not harmful by the quality oracle.}
%every time parameter $\poly(\secp) \leq t \leq 2^{O(\secp)}$, 
every set of harmful prompts $S_H \subseteq \{0,1\}^{l_m}$, where $l_m = \Theta(p(\secp)), \Omega(p(\secp)) = \log(|S_H|) \leq l_m - \Omega(p(\secp))$, 
for every language model $M$ running in time $\poly(\secp)$ per token and satisfying $H(M) \geq \Omega(p(\secp))$, and every harmfulness oracle $\mathcal{H}$ the following conditions hold.

There exists an adversarial language model $M'$ with a running time $\poly(\secp)$ per token and a harmfulness oracle $\mathcal{H}'$ such that the following conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item{\textbf{($M'$ outputs are harmful as judged by $\mathcal{H}'$)}.} For every malicious prompt $m \in S_H$, with all but negligible in $\secp$ probability over $z \gets M'(m)$,\footnote{Note that $M'(m)$ abuses the notation slightly. $M'$ is a malicious model that can, for every $m$, generate a harmful output with $m$ \say{hidden} in it. However, $m$ \emph{is not} an input/prompt to $M'$ in the usual sense.} it holds that
    \[
    \mathcal{H}'(z) = \Omega \Big(
    \mathbb{E}_{z \gets M(m)}[\mathcal{H}(z)] \Big).
    \]
    In words, for every prompt from $S_H$ the outputs of $M'$ are almost always as harmful (when judged by $\mathcal{H}'$) as if $M$ run on $m$ (when judged by $\mathcal{H}$).
    \item{\textbf{($\mathcal{H}'$ and $\mathcal{H}$ are equal on $M()$ almost always)}.} With all but negligible probability in $\secp$ over $z \gets M()$\footnote{We emphasize that $M()$ is \emph{not} a typo. $M()$ can be thought of as $G$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-output-formal} that also doesn't receive any input.}
    \[
    \mathcal{H}'(z) = \mathcal{H}(z).
    \]
    In words, the harmfulness of outputs of $M$ is (almost always) equal as judged by $\mathcal{H}$ and $\mathcal{H}'$.
    \item{\textbf{($M'$ and $M$ are indistinguishable for filters running in $2^{\eps \cdot \secp}$)}.} Under the assumption that RSW is secure, for every output filter running in time $2^{\eps \cdot \secp}$, for every $m \in S_H$ the advantage in distinguishing outputs of $M()$ and $M'(m)$ is negligible in $\secp$.

    In words, no filter running in time $2^{\eps \cdot \secp}$ can reject\footnote{If it doesn't also reject the innocent prompts.} the harmful outputs from $M'$.
\end{enumerate}

\end{theorem}

\subsection{Construction}

As we explained in Section~\ref{sec:overview} the construction is a \say{lift} from the construction given in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock} (Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock-formal} in the formal version).
More concretely we define
\begin{align*}
&M' := \mathsf{HarmIncite}(M,m,t = 2^\secp, \poly(\secp))  \\
&\mathcal{H}' := \mathsf{MaliciousLLM}(\mathcal{H},z,M,t = 2^\secp,\poly(\secp),S_H).
\end{align*}
We note that $\mathcal{H'}$ is well defined because the only calls to $\mathcal{H}$ in $\mathsf{MaliciousLLM}$ (Algorithm~\ref{alg:maliciousllmformal}) are query calls.
By direct, we mean that, for example, there is no assumption that $\mathcal{H}$ needs to be a language model.

\begin{remark}
We note that we could have set $t$ to a different (smaller) value and then the theorem would still hold with the security against filters running in time $t^\eps$.
This change would make sure that the \say{running time} of $\mathcal{H}'$ is smaller than $2^\secp$.
It can be beneficial if one tries to make sure that $\mathcal{H}'$ is efficient.
Thus our construction can be easily changed to have a trade-off between the overhead \say{running-time} (over $\mathcal{H}$) of $\mathcal{H}'$ and the security guarantee.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Proof}

The proof is a direct adaptation of the proof from Section~\ref{sec:mainformalproof}.
The main difference is in the setting of parameters for the time bounds.
In Theorem~\ref{thm:detection-impossible-timelock-formal} we needed $M'$ to be able to solve the time-lock puzzle (evaluate $r^{2^t}$) and so the indistinguishability could hold only against filters running in time $t^\eps$.
However, for the output filter result, the quality oracle could \say{run} in exponential time, and so the time-lock can be created with parameter $2^\secp$.\footnote{It cannot be \say{much} bigger because the running time of $M'$ is $\poly(\secp)$ and so $M'$ cannot create a harder puzzle (at least with the RSW construction).}

