\subhead{Overview}
% {\faissal As depicted in Figure 3, when} a new threat report is released, we use it as an input to our solution. 
\sol, depicted in~Fig.~\ref{fig:sol}, has four main steps: 
% 
\begin{enumerate*}[(1)]
    \item text pre-processing: involve standardizing the text to be ready for testflow extraction. % by removing unnecessary characters, correcting spelling errors, and converting all letters to lowercase. 
    %This preparation is essential for the efficiency of the subsequent steps.
    % the first step is text processing and it involves standardizing the text, which includes removing unnecessary characters such as extra spaces, removing spelling mistakes, and converting all letters to lower case to standardize the data. This preparation is essential to ensure the efficiency of the following steps.
    \item contextual analysis: interpret its context using semantics and word relationships. Language model (\eg BERT) is used to extract relevant information, detect subtexts, and identify key elements such as named entities and action verbs that are crucial for generating relevant testflows.
    % Once the text has been pre-processed, we pass it to the second phase which is contextual analysis. This step aims at understanding and interpreting the context of the text based on semantics and word relationships. To do this, we use advanced techniques such as LLM, which allows us to extract relevant information and detect subtexts or meanings in the sentences. By performing this analysis, we can identify key elements that are important for generating relevant testflows by recognizing named entities and identifying action verbs.
    \item sequence analysis: the identified entities and their context are analyzed to reconstruct the sequence and logic of the attack steps, providing a clearer understanding of the attack pattern and assisting in the testflow generation.
    \item The final step is testflow generation. Using insights from the contextual analysis, we automatically build testflows.  
    {\ahmadou These are generated using LM guided by rules trained on the analyzed data, resulting in a concrete list of testflows for the APT attack sequences listed in the report. To improve this process, we further include a testflow validator to filter out invalid or redundant testflows and refine those that can be adjusted to meet validity criteria.}
 %    These are created with LM, guided by rules trained on the analyzed data, resulting in a concrete list of testflows for the APT's attack sequences listed in the report.
    % % Finally, the last step in our process is testflow generation. Using the information and analysis from the contextual analysis, we build automated testflow. For these testflows, we use LLM based on a set of rules trained on the analyzed data. The output is the concrete list of testflows that will be used to evaluate our systems.
\end{enumerate*}

