Fig.~\ref{fig:fg_against_off_the_shelf} shows the comparison results of the testflows generated by \sol and Off-the-Shelf models across the five APT41 reports. We can see that \sol generates more than twice the number of valid testflows compared to {\tt ChatGPT-off-the-shelf} and {\tt Llama-off-the-shelf} across all reports. This demonstrates \sol's superior ability to produce valid testflows and underscores the significance of fine-tuning \sol with a customized NER model. % to extract specific cybersecurity-related entities and action verbs. 
In contrast, general-purpose LLMs, such as {\tt ChatGPT-off-the-shelf} and {\tt Llama-off-the-shelf}, lack accuracy in this specialized context.
% 
More specifically, \sol consistently achieves higher coverage rates compared to off-the-shelf models. For example, in Mandiant \#1 and Mandiant \#2, \sol achieves nearly three times the coverage of {\tt ChatGPT-off-the-shelf} and {\tt Llama-off-the-shelf}. However, in Mandiant \#3, Mandiant \#4, and TrendMicro \#5, the coverage rates are almost identical across all models. These results highlight that \sol not only generates the highest number of testflows but also aligns closely with the practices of manual practitioners. %, achieving an optimal balance between quantity and coverage.
