\begin{abstract} 
Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) has emerged as a popular and efficient alternative to reward modeling and reinforcement learning for aligning language models with human preferences. Despite its empirical success, the theoretical properties and intrinsic limitations of DPO remain underexplored. In this work, we first present a comprehensive analysis of DPO's dynamics from a probability evolution perspective. Our analysis reveals that DPO is highly sensitive to initialization. It also tends to misallocate probability mass, which can inadvertently shift probability toward irrelevant or undesired responses. This misallocation may unintentionally reinforce model bias, thereby compromising both the stability of model alignment and the consistency with intended preferences. Motivated by these theoretical findings, we propose a theoretically grounded bilevel optimization framework that tightly integrate supervised fine-tuning with an enhanced DPO objective a.k.a. stable preference optimization. Our approach introduces a principled regularization scheme to explicitly encourage absolute probability improvement for preferred outputs, while maintaining stable optimization dynamics. Experiments on challenging reasoning and summarization benchmarks elucidate that our method consistently improves reasoning accuracy and better aligns output distributions with intended preferences, outperforming standard DPO. Stable preference optimization provides new insights into the design of preference-based alignment objectives and opens up new avenues towards more reliable and interpretable language model alignment. 
\end{abstract}




\section{Introduction}
The recent surge of large language models (LLMs)~\citep{guo2025deepseek,qwq32b,jaech2024openai,grattafiori2024llama}, has significantly advanced the field of Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), demonstrating strong capabilities in complex reasoning tasks~\citep{sprague2024cot,xu2025towards}. In practice, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and preference-based alignment have become central strategies for steering LLM behavior toward human intent. While reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)~\citep{ouyang2022training,ziegler2019fine,stiennon2020learning,schulman2017proximal} is a widely used paradigm for preference alignment, it often requires complex reward modeling and substantial computational resources. To address these challenges, Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)~\citep{rafailov2023direct} has emerged as a popular method for aligning models with human preferences, due to its simplicity and effectiveness in leveraging pairwise preference data without the need for explicit reward modeling or reinforcement learning. By optimizing models to favor preferred responses over less preferred ones, DPO has demonstrated promising empirical results across a variety of language generation tasks, such as reasoning\citep{lai2024step, wang2023making,pang2024iterative} and alignment\citep{tunstall2023zephyr}.

Despite its widespread adoption and empirical success, DPO exhibits limitations that can compromise alignment quality and robustness~\citep{yuan2024advancing,rafailov2024r,tajwar2024preference}. Prior research has demonstrated that the probabilities of preferred and dispreferred responses may decrease simultaneously during DPO training\citep{pal2024smaug,rafailov2024r,tajwar2024preference,liu2024provably}, a phenomenon referred to as likelihood displacement~\citep{razin2024unintentional}. Existing work attributes this phenomenon to factors such as limited model capacity\citep{tajwar2024preference}, suboptimal initialization of the SFT model\citep{rafailov2024r}, or the characteristics of the input itself, as discussed in~\citet{pal2024smaug}. While \citet{razin2024unintentional} provide a theoretical analysis of this phenomenon, their results are derived under the assumption of single-token outputs, which limits their applicability to more general settings.

To address these challenges, we first conduct a comprehensive theoretical analysis of DPO’s optimization behaviors, focusing on the probability change dynamics under gradient updates. Different from prior works such as \citet{pal2024smaug,razin2024unintentional}, which focus on specific failure modes under settings like single token outputs or highly similar completions, and \citet{feng2024towards}, which analyze DPO from the perspective of the gradient field without directly quantifying probability dynamic changes, our analysis investigates probability evolutions for preferred and dispreferred samples without making strong assumptions. Our analysis reveals key limitations, such as DPO's sensitivity to initialization and its tendency to misallocate probability mass, which can hinder the alignment of LLMs. Building on these insights, we propose a principled bilevel optimization framework that unifies supervised fine-tuning and preference optimization within a single bilevel objective, rather than treating them as separate stages. %The lower-level SFT provides strong initialization, while the upper-level enhanced DPO objective further aligns model preferences and mitigates probability drift. This unified optimization approach enables more stable and effective alignment.

In summary, our contributions are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}

    \item \textbf{Theoretical Analysis of DPO}. We conduct a comprehensive and rigorous theoretical analysis of DPO's optimization behavior without strong assumptions, in contrast to prior works such as \citet{razin2024unintentional,pal2024smaug}. We analyze its properties through the lens of probability change dynamics, \(\Delta \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\) and \(\Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\), for preferred \(y_w\) and dispreferred  \(y_l\) responses. Our analysis reveals DPO's sensitivity to initialization, its inability to consistently increase the probability of preferred outputs, and its tendency to redistribute probability mass away from preferred responses, potentially increasing the probability of undesired outputs.

    \item \textbf{Stable Preference Optimization.} To address these limitations, we propose a theoretically grounded bilevel optimization method inspired by meta-learning\citep{franceschi2018bilevel,hospedales2021meta}. In this framework, the lower-level supervised fine-tuning provides a strong initialization, while the upper-level enhanced DPO objective with a gradient-based regularizer to explicitly encourage increasing the probability of preferred responses and to mitigate probability misallocation. By leveraging this principles to couple the two levels of optimization, our unified framework substantially improves the stability and effectiveness of direct alignment learning.


\end{enumerate}


\section{DPO Probability Evolution}

\subsection{Preliminaries}
Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a recently proposed method for aligning LLMs with human preferences without the need for explicit reward modeling or reinforcement learning fine-tuning~\citep{rafailov2023direct}. Given a dataset of preference triples $\mathcal{D} = \{(x_i, y_{w,i}, y_{l,i})\}_{i=1}^N$, where $y_{w,i}$ is the preferred response and $y_{l,i}$ is the dispreferred response for prompt $x_i$, DPO seeks to optimize the policy model $\pi_\theta$ such that the probability of generating $y_{w,i}$ increases relative to $y_{l,i}$.

The DPO loss function is defined as follows
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dpo}}(\theta; \mathcal{D}) = \mathbb{E}_{(x, y_w, y_l) \sim \mathcal{D}} \left[ -\log \sigma \left( \beta \log \frac{\pi_\theta(y_w|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_w|x)} - \beta \log \frac{\pi_\theta(y_l|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_l|x)} \right) \right],
\end{equation}
where $\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is a reference policy (often the initial SFT model), $\beta$ is a regularization parameter, and $\sigma(\cdot)$ is the sigmoid function.

\subsection{Probability Update Dynamics}

In this subsection, we rigorously analyze the update dynamics of DPO on the probability of preferred and dispreferred samples, laying the theoretical foundation for understanding its optimization behavior.

\begin{theorem}[Probability Update for Preferred and Dispreferred Samples]\label{thm:prob_change}
Let $(x, y_w, y_l)$ be a preference triple from the dataset, where $y_w$ is the preferred response and $y_l$ is the dispreferred response. After one gradient step of DPO with learning rate $\eta$, the changes in the probabilities $\pi_\theta(y_w|x)$ and $\pi_\theta(y_l|x)$ is given by
\begin{align} \label{eq:delta_pi_y_w}
\Delta_w &= \eta\beta (1-\sigma(z)) \pi_\theta(y_w|x) \left(\|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\|^2 - \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x) \cdot \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\right), 
\end{align}
\begin{align}\label{eq:delta_pi_y_l}
\Delta_l &= \eta\beta (1-\sigma(z)) \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \left(\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \cdot \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x) - \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\|^2\right),
\end{align}
where $\Delta_w =\Delta\pi_\theta(y_w|x)$, $\Delta_l =\Delta\pi_\theta(y_w|x)$, $z = \beta\log \frac{\pi_\theta(y_w|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_w|x)} - \beta\log \frac{\pi_\theta(y_l|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_l|x)}$ and $\eta\beta (1-\sigma(z)) \pi_\theta(y_l|x)>0$. The detailed proof can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:A.1}.
\end{theorem}


\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:prob_change} shows that the initial probabilities \(\pi_\theta(y_w|x)\) and \(\pi_\theta(y_l|x)\) determine the magnitude of the probability changes for the preferred and dispreferred outputs, while the similarity between their log-probability gradients determines the direction of these changes. In fact, \(\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x) \cdot \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\) is a neural tangent kernel~\citep{arora2019exact,jacot2018neural}, which can roughly characterize similarity between \(y_w\) and \(y_l\) based on the model's representation at time t and can change over LLM training steps. If the preferred sample \(y_w\) and the dispreferred sample \(y_l\) are similar, then the gradient term may be less than 0, i.e, the preferred sample's probability will decrease, combining with the Corollary~\ref{corollary:relative_change} that the preferred sample's probability will decrease more than the dispreferred sample's probability, which validates the previous empirical results\citet{pal2024smaug,razin2024unintentional}. This reveals that DPO’s optimization behavior depends heavily on both the initial response probabilities and the geometric alignment of the output space.
% The neural tangent kernel can be change over traini step\cite{}
% , which may potentially leads to unintended probability decreases for the preferred sample if the gradients are closely aligned.
\end{remark}

