Based on Theorem~\ref{thm:prob_change}, we provide the following two corollaries to further analyze the probability dynamic of preferred and dispreferred samples.

\begin{corollary}[Bound on Preferred and Dispreferred Probability Update]\label{corollary:bound_on_probability_change}
Under the same setting as Theorem~\ref{thm:prob_change}, the change in preferred probability $\Delta \pi_\theta(y_w|x)$ and dispreferred probability $\Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\Delta_w
\geq \eta\beta
(1-\sigma(z)) \pi_\theta(y_w|x) \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\| \left(\|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\| - \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\| \right),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
% resizebox{0.92\width}{!}{$
% \resizebox{0.92\textwidth}{!}{$
\begin{aligned}\label{eq:bound_on_dispreferred}
\Delta_l \leq \eta\beta (1 - \sigma(z)) \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\| \left( \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\| - \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\| \right),
\end{aligned}
% $}
\end{equation}
where \(\Delta_w =\Delta \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\) and \(\Delta_l =\Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\). The proof is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:A.2}.
\end{corollary}



\begin{remark}
Corollary~\ref{corollary:bound_on_probability_change} quantifies the conditions for probability changes in both preferred and dispreferred samples. When $|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)| > |\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)|$, the lower bound for $\Delta \pi_\theta(y_w|x)$ is positive, ensuring an increase in the probability of the preferred response. In this case, the upper bound for $\Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x)$ remains positive, which restricts but does not guarantee a decrease in the dispreferred probability. Conversely, if $|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)| < |\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)|$, the dispreferred probability is guaranteed to decrease, while the preferred probability may not necessarily increase. Thus, DPO may lead to undesirable outcomes, such as increasing the probability of dispreferred responses or decreasing that of preferred responses. To alleviate the risk of increasing dispreferred probabilities, according to Eq.~\eqref{eq:bound_on_dispreferred}, a constraint \(\|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\| - \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\|\leq0\) should be added to the optimization objective. It should be noted, however, that this constraint primarily serves as a safeguard: violating it does not necessarily result in performance degradation, as long as the optimization process continues to increase the preference for the desired samples and maintains or enlarges the margin between preferred and dispreferred outputs. The ablation study on the hyperparameter \(\gamma\) presented in Appendix~\ref{app:C.4} further supports this finding.
\end{remark}


\begin{corollary}
    [Non-Negativity of Relative Probability Change]\label{corollary:relative_change}
Under the same setting, the difference in probability changes between the preferred and dispreferred samples satisfies
\begin{align}
\Delta \geq \eta 
(1-\sigma(z)) \left\| \sqrt{\pi_\theta(y_w|x)} \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x) - \sqrt{\pi_\theta(y_l|x)} \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \right\|^2,
\end{align}
where \(\Delta=\Delta \pi_\theta(y_w|x) - \Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\). The proof is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:A.3}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Corollary~\ref{corollary:relative_change} demonstrates that DPO always guarantees a non-negative relative improvement of the preferred sample over the dispreferred one. However, this does not imply an absolute increase in the probability of the preferred sample, as both probabilities can decrease simultaneously. This phenomenon can result in undesirable behaviors, where neither the preferred nor the dispreferred sample is favored during training. For example, if the probability change for the preferred sample is negative ($\Delta_w < 0$), the dispreferred sample decreases even more ($\Delta_l < \Delta_w$) according to Corollary~\ref{corollary:relative_change}, so both probabilities can decrease as long as their margin increases, which may result in suboptimal alignment despite an increased relative margin.
\end{remark}

Figure~\ref{fig:log_delta_ratio} presents the log ratio \(\log \frac{\Delta \pi(y_w)}{\Delta \pi(y_l)}\) over steps, quantifying the relative log-probability changes between the chosen and rejected samples. The ratio remains positive and increases over steps, which indicates that the probability change for the preferred sample \( y_w \) consistently exceeds that of the dispreferred sample \( y_l \), even when both probabilities decline as observed in Figure~\ref{fig:log_probs}. This trend aligns with Corollary~\ref{corollary:relative_change}, which guarantees \(\Delta \pi_\theta(y_w|x) - \Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \geq 0\). In the next subsection, we will theoretically analyze where the reduced probability mass flows, i.e., which outputs gain probability.

\subsection{Probability Mass Shift}

\begin{theorem}[Probability Mass Shift]\label{thm:mass_shift}
For an input \( x \) with output space \( \mathcal{Y} \), let \( y_w \), \( y_l \), and \( y^* \) be the preferred, dispreferred, and the highest probability outputs, respectively. In a single DPO gradient update
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If \( \Delta \pi_\theta(y_w|x) < 0 \), satisfying \( \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x)\|^2 < \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x) \cdot \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \), then \( \Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \leq 0 \), and probability mass primarily flows to \( y^* \), i.e., \( \Delta \pi_\theta(y^*|x) > 0 \).
    \item If \( \Delta \pi_\theta(y_l|x) < 0 \), satisfying \( \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x) \cdot \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_w|x) < \|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log \pi_\theta(y_l|x)\|^2 \), then probability mass flows to \( y_w \) or \( y^* \), depending on their initial probabilities and gradient similarities.
\end{enumerate}
Proof can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:A.4}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:mass_shift} reveals that in DPO stage, probability mass may unexpectedly flow to the other non-preferred outputs (such as \( y^* \)), particularly those with high initial probability when the preferred sample probability decreases, or when the dispreferred sample probability decreases without sufficiently favoring the preferred sample. These potential failure modes of DPO can lead to unintended outputs, such as repetitive or biased responses, as reported by ~\citet{holtzman2019curious}. This effect highlights the importance of maintaining a well-initialized model and motivates the use of additional regularization or sophisticated optimization strategies to prevent unintended response probability increases for non-preferred responses during DPO.
\end{remark}

To empirically investigate Theorem~\ref{thm:mass_shift}, we conduct an experiment using the query ``\( 11 \times 12 = ? \)" (correct answer: 132). We sample 500 responses from \texttt{Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct} to construct a dataset, selecting the top 5 highest-probability correct responses (forming the preferred set \( \mathcal{D}_w \)) and 5 incorrect responses (forming the dispreferred set \( \mathcal{D}_l \), excluding \( y^* \), where \( y^* = \arg\max_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} \pi_\theta(y|x) \) is the highest-probability output from the SFT stage, not preferred). We train with DPO on this dataset and monitor the average log-probability changes of \( \mathcal{D}_w \), \( \mathcal{D}_l \), and \( y^* \). The results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:thm2}.
