\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Preference optimization for LLM alignment}. In LLM aligment, a widely adopted paradigm is reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)\citep{ziegler2019fine,stiennon2020learning,ouyang2022training}, where a reward model is first trained on preference data and then used to optimize the LLMs via reinforcement learning. While RLHF has proven effective at improving generation quality, it introduces significant computational overhead and can be unstable in practice\citep{zheng2023secrets,razin2024vanishing,ramamurthy2023reinforcement}. Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)\citep{rafailov2023direct} and its variants\citep{meng2024simpo,pal2024smaug,liu2024provably,xu2024contrastive,hong2024orpo,ethayarajh2024kto} address these issues by directly maximizing the log-probability ratio of preferred over dispreferred responses, without explicit reward modeling. Despite their efficiency and empirical success, the theoretical properties and potential limitations of DPO-based methods remain underexplored, motivating further analysis of their optimization behavior and failure modes.

\textbf{Likelihood Displacement in DPO.} DPO is prone to likelihood displacement during training\cite{razin2024unintentional}, a phenomenon that has recently attracted increasing theoretical attention. \citet{pal2024smaug} demonstrate that DPO may unintentionally reduce the probability of preferred responses, especially when completions are similar. \citet{feng2024towards} analyze the gradient field of DPO, highlighting its strong dependence on SFT initialization and its tendency to prioritize decreasing the likelihood of dispreferred outputs. \citet{razin2024unintentional} further show that, in the single-token setting, DPO can shift probability mass toward undesirable outputs. While several DPO variants have been proposed to mitigate these issues~\citep{pal2024smaug,meng2024simpo,xu2024contrastive,ethayarajh2024kto,hong2024orpo}, a comprehensive analysis of DPO's probability evolution in general setting remains lacking. \textit{Our work addresses this gap by providing a rigorous theoretical investigation and introducing a principled bilevel optimization framework to improve standard DPO.}


\section{Conclusion}

We presented a comprehensive analysis of DPO from a probability evolution perspective, theoretically revealing several key limitations: sensitivity to initialization, misallocation of probability mass, and the possibility of simultaneously decreasing the probabilities of both preferred and dispreferred responses. To address these issues, we proposed a principled bilevel optimization framework, SPO, that unifies supervised fine-tuning and preference optimization. Experimental results substantiate our theoretical claims, showing that our approach delivers more stable direct alignment learning and notable gains in both reasoning accuracy and summarization quality. Our work offers new insights into the mechanisms of direct preference learning, paving the way for more reliable and interpretable language model alignment.

