\subsection{Effect of Hyperparameters} \label{app:C.4}

\textbf{Effect of hyperparameter \(\lambda\).} We conduct an ablation study on the regularization hyperparameter \(\lambda\) using the GSM8K dataset and the \texttt{Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct} model. All experiments are initialized from the SFT checkpoint and run for 1000 steps with a batch size of 2 on a single RTX 4090 24GB GPU. The results are summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:lambda_ablation}.

When \(\lambda\) is small, the penalty for violating the constraint is weak, and the model behaves similarly to vanilla DPO, resulting in limited performance gains. As \(\lambda\) increases, the regularization effect strengthens, promoting better preference alignment and leading to notable improvements in accuracy. However, when \(\lambda\) becomes too large, the optimization can become overly constrained, potentially impeding the model's ability to balance the main objective and the regularization term, which leads to a slight drop in performance and may reduce generalization ability. These results suggest that tuning \(\lambda\) is important for achieving optimal trade-off between enforcing the constraint and maintaining overall model performance.

