\section{Related Work}

Deploying HAR models on edge devices has spurred research into efficiency-oriented architectures. A common strategy involves replacing standard convolutions with depthwise separable convolutions\cite{zhu2021lightweight, bian2024device}.
%, which reduce parameters and MACs while preserving spatial feature learning. 
%For instance, \cite{li2023action} demonstrated that such lightweight CNNs equipped with channel and temporal attention could rival larger models in accuracy. 
Recent studies \cite{lalwani2024novel,batool2024ensemble} also suggest that traditional recurrent networks remain popular for temporal modeling in HAR due to their strong ability to capture sequential dependencies. However, their computational overhead has motivated alternatives such as temporal pooling \cite{song2018deep} and dilated convolutions \cite{hamad2021dilated, bian2025hybrid}. 
Work like \cite{kang2025human} proposed pruning GRU layers to reduce latency, but this often sacrifices temporal granularity critical for fine-grained activity recognition. 
Beyond architectural innovations, techniques like quantization \cite{yi2023human}, knowledge distillation \cite{deng2023lhar}, and neural architecture search (NAS) \cite{lim2023efficient} have been applied for efficient HAR. 
%For example, HAR-NAS \cite{wang2021harnas} automated the design of tiny HAR models, though such methods require substantial computational resources for training. 
%Since such techniques can be applied to any architecture, this work will not discuss the benefits they can bring, but will only focus on the network architecture design. 
Recent work from KIT introduced TinyHAR \cite{zhou2022tinyhar}, a lightweight architecture that combines temporal convolutional blocks, self-attention mechanisms, LSTM layers, and temporal aggregation to optimize both spatial-temporal feature extraction and efficiency. TinyHAR achieves competitive accuracy while significantly reducing model size and computational costs.
%compared to classical frameworks like DeepConvLSTM \cite{ordonez2016deep}, setting a new benchmark for edge-compatible HAR systems. 
Despite advancements in recent lightweight HAR research, a few critical domain knowledge remain unaddressed: (1) Insufficient Methodological Rigor: prior works lack granular analyses of architectural component efficacy, impeding evidence-based model design; (2) Limited Robustness: existing architectures exhibit weak generalizability explorations across heterogeneous sensor configurations and activity taxonomies. (3) Inadequate Scalability: considering diverse edge-device constraints, current approaches seldom systematically explore scalable architectures that balance accuracy and efficiency.
